    },
    {
      "number": 556,
      "title": "concerto: fix font loading and extract release script",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nFixes Concerto font registration to work across all build contexts (release .app, `swift run`, xcodegen) by searching for the Fonts directory at runtime instead of relying on `Bundle.module` with `#if SWIFT_PACKAGE` compile-time switching.\n\nAlso extracts the release build/sign/notarize logic into a standalone `scripts/release-concerto.py` so CI can call it directly without routing through `concerto-dev.py`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Font loading**: Replace `Bundle.module` / `Bundle.main` conditional with a `findFontsDirectory()` search that checks SPM resource bundles in `Contents/Resources/`, adjacent to the executable, and directly in `Resources/`\n- **Release script**: Move release logic from `concerto-dev.py` into `scripts/release-concerto.py` as a standalone script; `concerto-dev.py release` now delegates to it\n- **CI**: Simplify release workflow step to call the new script directly, removing the `working-directory` override",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 249,
      "deletions": 179,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 557,
      "title": "lf land: keep rotated branches fresh after squash merge",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf wt list    # rotated branches show as \"fresh\", not \"squash-merged\"\nlf wt prune   # rotated branches are no longer incorrectly pruned\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAfter `lf land` squash-merges a branch and rotates to a new one, the new branch's tree is identical to main — so it was incorrectly flagged as \"squash-merged\" and prunable. This meant freshly-rotated branches could be immediately pruned, losing the user's working context.\n\nThe fix redefines \"fresh\" to include branches that are tree-equal to main even if they have commits (the squash-merged-parent case), and prevents squash-merged branches from being marked prunable while they're still fresh. Network enrichment only promotes squash-merged branches to prunable once they lose their fresh status (e.g., after a PR merge is confirmed).\n\n## Changes\n\n- Reworked `fresh` and `prunable` logic in `list_worktrees_local` so freshness takes precedence over squash-merged status\n- Extracted `apply_network_enrichment` from `enrich_worktrees_network` for testability\n- `wt list` and `wt prune` now sync main before listing to ensure accurate merge detection\n- Display logic prioritizes \"fresh\" over \"squash-merged\" in status output\n- Added unit test for network enrichment and integration test for the land-rotation scenario",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 117,
      "deletions": 16,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 558,
      "title": "lf land: stage uncommitted changes and fix stacked branch rebase after squash merge",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Stacked branches now rebase cleanly after their parent is squash-merged\nlf ops rebase  # automatically detects and skips squash-merged parent commits\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen branch B is stacked on branch A, and A gets squash-merged into main, rebasing B onto main would replay A's commits and hit conflicts. This PR adds fork-point detection using `git cherry` to identify commits already absorbed into the target, then uses `rebase --onto` to skip them.\n\nAlso fixes `rotate_worktree` to check wave items from the feature branch worktree (where update-wave runs) instead of from main, where shipped items have already been removed.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `squash_merge_fork_point()` in `git.rs` — uses `git cherry` to find the last commit whose patch is already in the target branch\n- `rebase_with_recovery` uses fork-point detection to skip squash-merged parent commits automatically\n- `rotate_worktree` reads wave items from the worktree (feature branch) instead of main repo\n- Integration test covering stacked branch rebase after squash merge\n- Terminology cleanup in wave docs: \"sprint\" → \"item\" throughout",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 145,
      "deletions": 38,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 559,
      "title": "concerto: detect local provider auth, eager daemon startup",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# Build and launch Concerto\nuv run python scripts/concerto-dev.py run-debug\n\n# Observe: daemon starts immediately at launch\n# Observe: provider connections detect local Claude/Codex auth from credential files\n```\n\nIf you have Claude Code authenticated (`~/.claude/.credentials.json` exists), the provider cards should show detected auth without clicking Connect.\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow extract_codex_token_reads_nested  # Rust: nested Codex token\nswift test --package-path swift --filter CredentialSocket  # Swift: file credential reader\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPhase 1 of the provider auth redesign. Instead of fake \"Connect\" buttons that open browser flows the app can't complete, detect credentials that already exist on disk (`~/.claude/.credentials.json`, `~/.codex/auth.json`, macOS Keychain). Instead of waiting for the user to open a repo window to start the daemon, start eagerly at app launch.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Eager daemon startup** — `SharedDaemon.eagerStart()` fires at app launch; later `start()` calls join the in-flight task instead of racing\n- **File-based credential detection** — new `FileCredentialReader` reads Claude and Codex auth files from disk before falling back to Keychain\n- **Nested Codex token support** — Rust and Swift both handle the `tokens.access_token` shape from ChatGPT auth mode\n- **Streamlined bundled-mode handshake** — localhost skips TLS trust checks and repo discovery; health poll interval tightened\n- **Simplified sidebar** — removed orphan worktree section; provider cards show detected/connected state\n- **Graceful dev shutdown** — `concerto-dev.py` force-kills the app and bundled daemon if quit is blocked by a modal\n- **Wave doc** — folded Phase 2+ auth redesign steps into the concerto wave plan",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 595,
      "deletions": 204,
      "changed_files": 21
    },
    {
      "number": 560,
      "title": "redesign: chords are waves, tend flow, wave configs",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# bootstrap the redesign wave structure\npython scripts/bootstrap-redesign.py\n\n# see the new tend flow\nlf flow tend\n\n# wave detail now shows direction and area\nlfq wave redesign\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe chord model collapses into the wave model. A chord-wave is now an ordinary wave whose `area` points at `wave/<name>/` directories — no separate chord tables, DTOs, routes, or client helpers. This eliminates ~1400 lines of chord-specific CRUD across Rust (store, HTTP, types), Python (client, api, models), and tests, replaced by the wave API doing the same job.\n\nOn top of that baseline, this PR adds the **tend flow** — the coordinating voice that lets a chord-wave observe, assess, and mutate its member waves. Five new steps (`scan-waves`, `assess`, `draft-chord`, `review-chord`, `apply-chord`) and two new flows (`tend`, `tend-chord`) wire into the existing flow engine.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Chord removal**: drop `chords` and `chord_members` tables (migration 028), delete `chords.rs` routes, remove `Chord` model and all chord client/API methods\n- **Tend flow**: `tend` (scan → assess → branch) and `tend-chord` (draft → review → apply) flows with five new step prompts under `steps/tend/`\n- **Wave configs**: `wave/` directory with redesign chord-wave and four member waves (chord-model, clear-the-deck, agent-embedding, signals), each with README, yaml config, and work items\n- **Client compression**: `_compact_dict` helper replaces repeated `if x is not None` payload building across all client methods\n- **CLI**: `lfq wave <name>` shows direction and area fields; `ship-roadmap` flow splits into branch(build, reorg)\n- **Roadmap**: `wave/roadmap.md` lays out phasing across bootstrap, build+tend, and chord-wave earning its keep\n- **Bootstrap script**: `scripts/bootstrap-redesign.py` registers wave configs against lfd",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2456,
      "deletions": 1387,
      "changed_files": 79
    },
    {
      "number": 562,
      "title": "lf pm: add PM integration foundation (Asana + Linear)",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlfq auth asana       # store Asana personal access token\nlfq auth linear      # store Linear API key\nlfq auth status      # shows Asana/Linear alongside existing providers\n```\n\nWave configs can now declare a PM provider:\n\n```yaml\n# wave/scan/scan.yaml\nflow: build\npm:\n  provider: asana\n  project: \"1234567890\"\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds the foundation for syncing waves with external PM tools (Asana and Linear). This wave introduces the core types, provider auth, config parsing, and CLI commands — everything needed before building the actual API clients and sync logic.\n\nPM providers are treated differently from model providers: they use API-key auth but don't show pay-per-token billing copy, since they aren't metered model calls.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `pm.rs` module with `PmProvider` trait, `PmConfig`, `PmItem` types, and `RoadmapItemDocument` frontmatter parser\n- `Provider` enum gains `Asana` and `Linear` variants with centralized auth metadata (`display_name`, `api_key_env_name`, `api_key_bills_per_token`)\n- CLI gets `lfq auth asana` / `lfq auth linear` commands; provider label/config maps deduplicated into module-level constants\n- `AsanaConfig` and `LinearConfig` added to engine config for workspace/team defaults\n- Wave config parses optional `pm:` block as `PmConfig`\n- Onboarding flow simplified by leveraging centralized provider metadata\n- Wave roadmap under `wave/pm/` with design docs for remaining work (clients, bootstrap CLI, steps, ingest, run lifecycle sync)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 882,
      "deletions": 179,
      "changed_files": 24
    },
    {
      "number": 563,
      "title": "attention queue: surface items that need human action",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo fmt --check && cargo clippy -- -D warnings && cargo test --all\nswift test --package-path swift\n```\n\nOpen Concerto without selecting a wave — the detail pane now shows the attention queue instead of the empty \"catch a wave\" state. Code review items expose a **Ship** button; step failures expose **Retry**.\n\n## Summary\n\nWaves generate attention items when they need a human: code reviews ready to ship, step failures, and queue blocks (rebase conflicts, missing PRs, dirty scratch). These items flow through a three-state lifecycle (surfaced → viewed → resolved) and auto-resolve when conditions clear (PR merges, run superseded, queue unblocked).\n\nThe attention queue replaces the old queue block storage with a unified model that covers both algedonic alerts and interactive steps, giving the UI a single sorted feed of everything that needs action.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Rust: `lfd::attention` module** — creation helpers, lifecycle transitions, reconciliation logic, and round-trip conversion between `QueueBlock` and `AttentionItem`\n- **Rust: HTTP routes** — `GET /attention`, `GET /attention/history`, `GET /attention/{id}`, `PATCH /attention/{id}` (marks viewed)\n- **Rust: store layer** — `attention_items` table (migration 029), upsert/get/list in both SQLite and Postgres\n- **Rust: queue + executor** — queue blocks now create/update/resolve attention items with event emission; wave executor creates attention on run completion and failure\n- **Swift: `AttentionItem` model + `AttentionStore`** — typed context enum (code review, queue failure, step failure), polling via `LocalWaveService`\n- **Swift: `AttentionQueueView`** — master-detail split with filter picker (all/steps/alerts), detail pane with contextual action buttons\n- **Swift: daemon eager start** — `BundledDaemonManager` starts on app launch so provider auth is available before a repo connects\n- **Swift: file credential reader** — reads Claude/Codex tokens from disk before falling back to Keychain\n- **Wave plan** — marks block queue view as shipped, refreshes remaining steps",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2316,
      "deletions": 217,
      "changed_files": 38
    },
    {
      "number": 564,
      "title": "pm asana: OAuth auth, client, and wave export",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ops auth asana       # connect Asana via OAuth (local credentials)\nlf ops auth status      # check local provider auth\nlf ops export pm        # push wave roadmap items to Asana project\nlfq auth asana          # connect Asana via OAuth (studio)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds the first PM integration: an Asana client that can read and write tasks, a local `lf ops auth` command that stores provider credentials without needing a running `lfd` studio, and an `lf ops export` command that syncs wave roadmap items to Asana projects. Asana auth moves from PAT-based to OAuth with authorization-code flow.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Asana client** (`lfd/pm/asana.rs`): full Asana API client with rate-limit retries, project/section/task CRUD, and workspace discovery\n- **PM provider trait** (`lfd/pm/mod.rs`): `PmProvider` abstraction over Asana (Linear next) with `PmItem` / `PmItemCreate` / `PmItemUpdate` types\n- **Local auth CLI** (`lf/commands/auth.rs`): `lf ops auth {connect,disconnect,status,configure}` — stores credentials in the local lfd SQLite store, no daemon required\n- **OAuth code flow**: Asana uses out-of-band redirect; CLI prompts for the authorization code and exchanges it via `complete_auth`\n- **Export command** (`ops/export.rs`): reads wave YAML + numbered markdown items, diffs against existing Asana tasks, creates/updates to match\n- **Python client/CLI**: `complete_auth` endpoint, `_extract_authorization_code` helper, Asana switched from `configure_api_key` to `_connect_provider`\n- **Wave PM roadmap**: updated with concrete codebase references and shipped status for the Asana wave item",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2901,
      "deletions": 263,
      "changed_files": 47
    },
    {
      "number": 565,
      "title": "flow: rename fork/branch to and/or, add router support and silence paths",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```yaml\n# or with explicit router step\n- tend/scan-waves\n- or:\n    router: tend/assess\n    paths:\n      tune:\n        flow: tend-tune\n        description: \"Adjustments needed\"\n      silence:\n        description: \"No action needed\"\n```\n\n```yaml\n# and (parallel execution)\n- and:\n    step: reduce\n    drafts:\n      - direction: infra\n      - direction: ux\n- update-wave\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nRenames the flow primitives from `fork`/`branch` to `and`/`or` — shorter names that match their semantics (parallel-all vs pick-one). The `or` construct gains two capabilities: a `router:` field that names an existing step as the routing agent (its prompt gets routing instructions appended automatically), and silence paths — paths with only a `description` and no `flow:`/`step:` that act as clean no-op exits.\n\nThe CLI flow runner can now execute `or` routing inline instead of requiring the daemon. Both CLI and daemon share `build_or_routing_suffix`, `read_or_verdict`, and `load_or_path_items` helpers.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Rename all Rust types: `Fork`→`And`, `Branch`→`Or`, `BranchDef`→`OrDef`, `BranchPath`→`OrPath`\n- Rename YAML keys: `fork:`→`and:`, `branch:`→`or:`\n- Add `router:` field to `or` — routes through a named step instead of a generic agent\n- Allow silence paths (no `flow:`/`step:`) as valid no-op exits\n- Wire `run_or` in the CLI flow runner with temp step cleanup via RAII guard\n- Verdict file moves from `scratch/route-branch.md` to `scratch/route-or.md`\n- `lf land`: check PR existence earlier, preserve `scratch/.gitkeep`, stage uncommitted changes\n- Rename tend flows: `tend-chord`→`tend-tune`, `ship-roadmap-build`→`ship-roadmap-play`\n- Expand `scan-waves` step to read runtime state via `lfq show`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 952,
      "deletions": 491,
      "changed_files": 31
    },
    {
      "number": 566,
      "title": "lfd auth: collapse to local and studio modes",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```yaml\n# ~/.lf/lfd.yaml\nauth:\n  mode: local   # or studio\n```\n\n```bash\n# env override\nexport LFD_AUTH_MODE=studio\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nRemoves the `static` auth provider and the `ci` alias, leaving two auth modes: `local` (session token on disk) and `studio` (session token on loopback, connection tokens for remote clients). Both modes now generate a session token at startup — `auth.token` / `LFD_AUTH_TOKEN` is an optional override for embedded launches rather than a required field.\n\nThis simplifies the auth surface for deployment: remote deploys use `studio` mode with `~/.lf/credentials.json` instead of managing a static bearer token. The `lfd token rotate` subcommand, the iOS manual connection screen, and all `LFD_AUTH_PROVIDER` references are removed.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Replace `auth.provider` string with `AuthMode` enum (`Local`, `Studio`) with serde deserialization, `deny_unknown_fields` to reject the old `provider` key, and a rejection test for the removed `ci` alias\n- Unify session token creation into `load_or_create_session_token` — both modes write `~/.lf/session-token`, with optional override from `auth.token`\n- Remove `AuthProvider::Static` variant and all `static`/`ci` code paths\n- Remove `lfd token rotate` subcommand and iOS `ConnectionSetupView`\n- Update deploy docs, docker-compose files, and `lfd.md` to reflect the two-mode model\n- Fold completed auth cleanup out of clear-the-deck backlog; move remaining items (deployment collapse, sandbox pause) up and add deferred auth items to trust wave",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 388,
      "deletions": 651,
      "changed_files": 33
    },
    {
      "number": 568,
      "title": "concerto: connections panel redesign + Doppler secrets provider",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow -- secrets          # 9 tests: sync, clear, status, smart defaults\nswift test --package-path swift            # SecretsProviderTests, SmartDefaultConfigTests\n```\n\nIn Concerto, open any repo's Connection Settings. Providers are now grouped by role (Agents, Source Control, Project Management, Secrets). Doppler appears under Secrets — connect via OAuth, pick a project and config, and watch Claude/Codex keys populate from Doppler. Portfolio window gets a Connections toolbar button that opens a sheet with the same grouped panel.\n\n## Summary\n\nTwo pieces that belong together: the secrets provider needed a home in the UI, and the connections panel needed structure. Grouping providers by role makes the panel scannable and gives secrets a natural location instead of a bolted-on section.\n\nDoppler is a first-class auth provider — its token lives in `provider_tokens` alongside GitHub, Claude, etc. `secrets_provider_config` stores only the project/config selection. If `doppler login` was run in a terminal, lfd auto-persists the token on the next status check.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Rust**: new `lfd::secrets` module with Doppler API client (projects, configs, fetch/sync/clear), HTTP routes at `/secrets/*`, migration 031 for `secrets_provider_config`, SSE events for connect/disconnect/sync\n- **Rust**: `DopplerAuthBroker` in `provider_auth` — OAuth flow via `doppler login`, auto-persist CLI tokens, status check via `doppler configure get token`\n- **Swift**: `SecretsProviderStore` with reactive state, `LocalWaveService` secrets methods, `ConnectionsPanel` with role-based provider grouping replacing the old `AuthProviderCard` list\n- **Swift**: `PortfolioConnectionsSheet` for portfolio-level access, `ConnectionSetupView` for iOS\n- **Tests**: 9 Rust unit tests, Swift `SecretsProviderTests` covering sync, disconnect, status, smart config defaults\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Removed `SecretsProvider` trait — only one implementation, direct functions are simpler\n- Separate browse/select API (`GET /secrets/projects`, `GET /secrets/configs?project=`, `POST /secrets/select`) so the UI can browse before committing\n- Only two key mappings today: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` → Claude, `OPENAI_API_KEY` → Codex",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2345,
      "deletions": 298,
      "changed_files": 32
    },
    {
      "number": 569,
      "title": "chord-model: algedonic signals — repair lineage, error classification, and escalation",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nRestructures the chord model around VSM (viable system model) and algedonic signals. When a wave run step fails, lfd now attempts headless repair in the same branch/worktree before escalating. If the repair attempt also fails, an algedonic signal surfaces in the attention queue for the parent chord (or the human, at root).\n\nThis replaces the previous trigger-based approach with the core cybernetic loop: detect failure → classify error → repair → escalate if stuck.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`WaveRun.repair_of: Option<LfdId>`** — new field linking a repair run to the run it's repairing. Persisted across sqlite/postgres with migration 030.\n- **`classify_repair_flow`** — examines a failed run and returns the appropriate repair flow (`debug` for everything; ci-fix failures get `debug` to avoid loops)\n- **`create_repair_run`** — creates a new run in the same worktree/branch with `repair_of` set\n- **`fail_run` dispatch** — only creates algedonic attention items when `repair_of` is set (repair already tried). First failures skip escalation to give repair a chance. Worktree cleanup deferred when repair will follow.\n- **Wave chord-model restructured** — `01-chord-triggers` and `02-tend-flow-steps` replaced with `01-algedonic-signals`, `02-vsm-flow`, `03-wave-discovery`. README rewritten around VSM mapping at two granularities (single-pass flow vs five-wave chord).\n- **Design doc** at `scratch/chord-model-algedonic.md` with the full error → repair → escalate sequence and CI failure proving slice\n- **`scripts/dev-lfq`** — dev helper for running lfd queue commands in isolation",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 482,
      "deletions": 111,
      "changed_files": 26
    },
    {
      "number": 570,
      "title": "redesign: attention queue, pm integration, auth collapse, wave reorganization",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# attention queue\ncurl localhost:7700/v0/attention              # list attention items\ncurl localhost:7700/v0/attention/:id/resolve   # resolve an item\n\n# pm integration (Asana)\nlf pm auth asana          # OAuth flow\nlf pm export-waves asana  # export waves to Asana\n\n# auth (simplified)\nLFD_AUTH_MODE=local lfd   # local token (default)\nLFD_AUTH_MODE=studio lfd  # studio OAuth\n\n# land now stages uncommitted changes\nlf land                   # auto-stages before landing\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nBootstraps the redesign chord: adds the attention queue as the primary human-decision surface, integrates PM tooling for external coordination, collapses auth to two honest modes, and reorganizes wave plans to match the new chord structure.\n\nThe attention queue (`AttentionItem`) is the shared model for code review, design review, calibration, and failure states — stored in sqlite/postgres with HTTP APIs, websocket updates, and a macOS queue home screen in Concerto. PM integration adds Asana OAuth and wave-to-project export so wave state can flow into external trackers. Auth drops the `ci` and `provider` modes in favor of just `local` and `studio`.\n\nOn the planning side, five old waves (foundation, trust, context, concerto, scale) are triaged into four redesign waves plus a dogfood wave and a new PM wave. Useful items are integrated, speculative items are dropped or saved in backlog.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Attention queue** — `AttentionItem` types, sqlite migration, HTTP routes (`/v0/attention`), reconciliation triggers, `AttentionQueueView` in Concerto, `AttentionStore` in LoopflowCore\n- **PM integration** — Asana OAuth client, wave export, `lf pm` CLI commands, Linear client step placeholder\n- **Auth collapse** — `auth.mode` accepts only `local`/`studio`, rejects `ci`/`provider`, session token path unified\n- **lf land** — stages uncommitted changes before landing\n- **lf auth** — new CLI auth commands in Rust\n- **Concerto** — `AttentionQueueView`, credential socket hardening, wave sidebar cleanup, `WorktreeRow` deleted, iOS `ConnectionSetupView` deleted\n- **Wave reorganization** — deleted `wave/concerto/`, `wave/context/`, `wave/foundation/`, `wave/scale/`, `wave/trust/`; created `wave/dogfood/`, `wave/pm/`; updated roadmap with triage and phasing",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 136,
      "deletions": 872,
      "changed_files": 35
    },
    {
      "number": 571,
      "title": "clear-the-deck: collapse lfd deploy docs and harden pr copy",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.lf\ncat > ~/.lf/lfd.yaml <<'YAML'\nmode: container\nYAML\n\nlfd install\nlf ops pr\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR finishes the current clear-the-deck pass around `lfd` deployment by making the blessed remote shape explicit: `mode: container` with Docker and studio auth. It updates docs and compose defaults to match that story, removes checked-in config noise, and makes PR title/body generation more reliable when agent output includes extra JSON-like logging. It also isolates config and PR/land tests from `$HOME` so results do not depend on the developer machine.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Make Docker compose and deploy docs treat container + studio auth as the default remote path, and point credential mounting at `LFD_EXECUTOR_CREDENTIALS_MOUNTS` / `executor.credentials.mounts` instead of hand-editing compose volumes.\n- Rewrite `docs/lfd.md` and `docs/getting-started.md` around the two supported deployment shapes, `native` and `container`, with `~/.lf/lfd.yaml` as the persistent mode selector.\n- Harden `lf ops pr` so generated PR copy can be parsed from `stdout`, `stderr`, or mixed noisy agent output, with tests for wrapped JSON and braces inside body strings.\n- Isolate config, PR, and land tests from ambient HOME state, trim repo-local `.lf/config.yaml` to shared settings, and narrow `wave/clear-the-deck` to the remaining sandbox-executor decision.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 709,
      "deletions": 1883,
      "changed_files": 34
    },
    {
      "number": 572,
      "title": "pm: add linear support and lf ops pm sync",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ops auth configure linear\nlf ops pm export pm\nlf ops pm import pm\nlf ops pm sync pm\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR turns PM integration into a provider-agnostic workflow instead of an Asana-only export path. It adds Linear support, introduces local `lf ops pm` import/export/sync commands, and reshapes wave metadata so the same roadmap files can track separate Asana and Linear IDs while using a repo-level default provider. It also tightens the surrounding auth and PR-copy automation so the new flow works cleanly in day-to-day use.\n\n## Changes\n\n- add a new `LinearClient` with GraphQL project, issue, comment, and completion support, plus shared retry handling and reusable PM test infrastructure\n- add `lf ops pm import`, `lf ops pm export`, and `lf ops pm sync`; export can create missing projects, import reconciles numbered wave items from the PM tool, and sync does a three-way merge against `main`\n- move PM config to a repo-level default provider and per-wave `asana_project` / `linear_project` fields, and switch roadmap frontmatter from a single `pm_id` to provider-specific IDs\n- generalize Asana export/update behavior to share the same provider plumbing as Linear, including noop filtering for rank-only updates\n- improve provider auth UX with shared OAuth code handling, Linear auth support, and expiry-aware polling in the Python CLI\n- make `lf ops pr` accept labeled agent output like `Title:` and `Body:` so Codex-style PR generation parses reliably\n- refresh PM wave docs and remove roadmap items that shipped during the Linear and Asana integration work",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3011,
      "deletions": 685,
      "changed_files": 66
    },
    {
      "number": 575,
      "title": "review: split into demo and code-review, reframe review-design",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ship          # review step now routes to demo or code-review\nlf ship-roadmap  # same routing after build\n```\n\nThe single `review` step tried to do two things: walk through observable behavior and evaluate code design. This splits it into two focused steps with an `or` branch that routes based on the nature of the change.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`demo` step** — experience-first walkthrough. Runs things, shows output, lets the human react before looking at code.\n- **`code-review` step** — structural/architectural walkthrough. Goes beyond the diff into surrounding code and codebase trajectory.\n- **`review-design` rewrite** — reframed from \"pressure-test the design\" to \"reshape the AI's elaboration into your actual intent.\" The agent presents its understanding and the human sculpts it.\n- **`review` step deleted** — replaced by the `demo | code-review` branch in `ship.yaml` and `ship-roadmap-play.yaml`\n- **`discovery.rs`** — updated step listing to reflect new steps\n- **README** — updated step and flow tables",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 744,
      "deletions": 251,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 578,
      "title": "lfd: ship algedonic signals with repair backoff",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/demo-algedonic.py --skip-build\nlf ops pr\nlf ops land --create-pr\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR makes algedonic signals live in `lfd`. Failed runs now go through a bounded repair chain with backoff before escalating into the attention queue, and the supporting plumbing was tightened so the flow works in real usage: local dev instances can isolate state with `LF_HOME`, PR metadata follows the active `WaveRun`, and PR copy generation is more tolerant of noisy agent output. The chord-model wave docs were updated to mark the feature shipped and leave live CI proof as the next validation step.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add repair-chain tracking in `lfd` with fixed backoff delays (`30s`, `60s`, `120s`) and algedonic escalation after the retry limit, plus tests for repair depth counting.\n- Move PR state from `WaveRunSnapshot` onto `WaveRun` and sync it after `lf ops land --create-pr` so webhook matching, live PR state, queue logic, and code-review attention use the current PR.\n- Honor `LF_HOME` for lfd config and test/dev setup so local runs do not share `~/.lf` state.\n- Harden `lf ops pr` parsing so it can recover title/body JSON from noisy, fenced, or loosely formatted agent responses.\n- Add `scripts/demo-algedonic.py` and update the chord-model roadmap/docs to prove the end-to-end repair and escalation path.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 720,
      "deletions": 194,
      "changed_files": 26
    },
    {
      "number": 579,
      "title": "ci: enforce clear scratch before landing",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ops pr land\ncargo test --test land_tests land_clears_scratch_and_preserves_gitkeep\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR stops the merge queue from silently rewriting PR branches to clean `scratch/` and instead makes an empty `scratch/` directory a normal CI requirement. It folds the check into the main CI workflow, adds it to the aggregated status job, and adds Rust coverage to confirm landing leaves `scratch/` cleared while preserving `.gitkeep`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add a `scratch-clear` job to `.github/workflows/ci.yml` that fails when `scratch/` contains anything other than `.gitkeep`\n- Include that job in the `tests-result` aggregator so the repo can treat scratch cleanliness like any other required check\n- Remove the separate `clear-scratch` merge-queue workflow that auto-committed cleanup back to the PR branch\n- Add a Rust test proving `land()` clears nested scratch files and keeps `scratch/.gitkeep` in place",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 124,
      "deletions": 52,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 580,
      "title": "redesign: fold signals into chord-model and clear scratch on land",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run lf -l | sed -n '/Tend/,+4p'\ncargo test -p loopflow render_pipeline_lines_expands_or_paths_on_separate_lines -- --nocapture\ncargo test -p loopflow summarize_tool_bash_truncates_on_char_boundary -- --nocapture\ncargo test -p loopflow --test land_tests land_clears_scratch_and_preserves_gitkeep -- --nocapture\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR narrows the redesign chord back to the waves that are actually carrying Phase 1 work, folds the useful parts of `signals` into `chord-model`, deletes the now-stale `clear-the-deck` wave, and records a full tend scan/assessment/chord route that chooses `tune`. In the same slice, `lf flow` gets a much clearer pipeline header for routed flows, tool summaries stop truncating on invalid UTF-8 boundaries, and landing/CI now treat leftover `scratch/` files as a failure instead of cleaning them up after the fact.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Trim the redesign roadmap from four member waves to two, update the chord-model docs to own algedonic, mutation, and memory follow-ons, and adjust the beat-synthesizer example to match the new active wave set.\n- Add `scratch/tend-scan.md`, `scratch/tend-assessment.md`, `scratch/tend-chord.md`, and `scratch/route-or.md` to capture the current redesign state and the reasoning behind routing this tend cycle to `tune`.\n- Render `lf flow` headers as multi-line trees for `and`/`or` branches, including router names and expanded nested paths, instead of flat `[and]` and `[or]` placeholders.\n- Make stream tool summaries truncate by character boundary, add regression coverage for the Unicode case, and add a `land` test proving `scratch/` is cleared back to `.gitkeep`.\n- Replace the merge-queue scratch auto-cleanup workflow with a normal CI check plus an aggregate result job so dirty `scratch/` content blocks landing early.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 222,
      "deletions": 538,
      "changed_files": 23
    },
    {
      "number": 581,
      "title": "ops wt: prefer exact branch matches and support sourced dev-lf",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ops wt switch my-feature\nlf ops wt switch jack.chord-model.20260316_1856\n\nsource scripts/dev-lf ops wt switch jack.chord-model.20260316_1856\n```\n\n`lf ops wt switch` now accepts either the short worktree name or the full branch name. When using the local dev wrapper, sourcing `scripts/dev-lf` applies generated shell directives like `cd` in the current shell instead of only printing them.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR tightens worktree switching so branch-qualified names resolve to the intended worktree before falling back to wave-name and path heuristics. That avoids ambiguous or incorrect matches for timestamped branches and custom branch naming schemas. It also makes `scripts/dev-lf` behave more like the real shell integration by supporting sourced execution, which lets local development flows apply directives directly while still printing guidance when run as a normal script.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Update `lf ops wt switch` to prefer an exact branch-name match before checking derived worktree names or directory names\n- Preserve the existing short-name behavior while preventing unrelated worktree-path reuse for branch names under custom schemas\n- Add Rust integration tests covering exact branch precedence, custom schema branch names, and failure on unrelated matches\n- Update CLI help and docs to document switching by full branch name\n- Rework `scripts/dev-lf` so it can be sourced, apply `lf` shell directives in-place, and print a tip when invoked without sourcing",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 236,
      "deletions": 51,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 584,
      "title": "chord model: rename tend→garden, add VSM governance flows, xor/or/loop engine",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf flow garden-or-silent    # scan → assess → xor(garden, silence)\nlf flow govern-control      # s3-scan → s3-assess → wave/mutate\nlf flow build               # kickoff → review-design → loop(code → xor(demo, code-review), exit: gate) → deploy\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nShips the chord model's governance layer. The old `tend` flow (scan → assess → draft-chord → review-chord → apply-chord) is replaced by two simpler composable pieces: `garden` (scan → assess → mutate → review) and four VSM governance flows at Beer's system levels (s2–s5). Each governance flow scans its domain, assesses, then feeds into the shared `wave/mutate` step.\n\nThe flow engine gains `xor:` (exactly-one branch, replacing the overloaded `or:` for routing), `or:` (multi-select — run one or more paths), and `loop:` (repeat body until exit condition). `ops:` is renamed to `op:` throughout.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Flow engine** (`flow.rs`): add `Xor`, `Or` (multi-select), and `Loop` flow item types with parsing, expansion, and rendering; collapse duplicated branch plumbing between xor and or\n- **Builtin flows**: reorganize `code/` into inner-loop building blocks, add `build/`, `garden/`, `algedonic/`, `vsm/` flow directories\n- **Builtin steps**: replace `tend/{scan-waves,assess,draft-chord,review-chord,apply-chord}` with `garden/{scan,assess}`, `wave/{mutate,review}`, and `vsm/s{2-5}-{scan,assess}`\n- **Wave executor**: integrate VSM governance flow execution, add pm sync hooks, drop `SessionManager` dep from `WaveExecutor`\n- **Python models**: parse `op:` prefix (keep `ops:` compat), add `[loop]` sentinel\n- **README**: updated flow tables, branch docs, and step reference to match new names\n- **Wave items**: fold shipped work into chord-model items, split `02-vsm-flow` into focused items (worker-pools, wave-modes, vsm-chord-configs, concurrent-ingest)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2293,
      "deletions": 1039,
      "changed_files": 89
    },
    {
      "number": 585,
      "title": "tend: improve scan visibility and rewrite chord review",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf tend\n```\n\nThe scan step now sees unlanded branches and local worktrees. The review step replaces mutation-by-mutation triage with a conversation about what's working and what isn't.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe tend cycle misdiagnosed a deadlock between chord-model and agent-embedding because it could only see main and open PRs — missing 37 commits of real progress on unlanded branches. Two fixes: the scan-waves step now checks remote branches and local worktrees for work ahead of main, and the review-chord step was rewritten to surface coherence rather than walk through mutations asking for verdicts.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `scan-waves.md`: added step 4 to scan remote branches (`git branch -r`) and local worktrees for unlanded work, plus an \"Unlanded Branches\" section in the output template\n- `.lf/steps/tend/review-chord.md`: new repo override — conversation-driven review that starts with what's working, finds the central tension, and produces a chord with only the moves that emerged as necessary\n- `scratch/` artifacts from the tend cycle: scan, assessment, chord, and application log",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 86,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 586,
      "title": "lf pm: bootstrap CLI with init, pull, and status commands",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ops pm init pm          # connect one wave project, link items, write IDs\nlf ops pm init --all       # bootstrap every wave/ project\nlf ops pm pull pm          # rewrite one wave from PM; remote wins\nlf ops pm pull --all       # rewrite every wave from PM\nlf ops pm status           # show linked waves and local/remote counts\n```\n\n```yaml\n# .lf/config.yaml\npm:\n  provider: linear\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the old import/export PM commands with a three-verb CLI: `init` connects a wave to its PM provider (creating projects and linking items by name), `pull` rewrites local wave files from the remote source of truth, and `status` shows sync state across all waves. The default provider switches from Asana to Linear.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`pm init`** — finds or creates a PM project for a wave, matches local items to remote by name, creates missing items, and writes PM IDs into wave YAML\n- **`pm pull`** — fetches remote items and rewrites local wave markdown files to match; remote ordering and content win\n- **`pm status`** — lists all waves with their provider, project ID, and local vs remote item counts\n- Deleted `ops/export.rs` and the old `export`/`import` subcommands; collapsed duplicated sync helpers into `ops/pm.rs`\n- Linear client gains `create_project`, `create_issue`, `update_issue_priority`, and `list_items` with correct priority ordering\n- `branch_rename` now handles worktree-checked-out branches by finding the worktree and renaming from inside it\n- Wave files renumbered and updated: shipped items deleted, accuracy fields refreshed across all wave projects\n- Config renames `PmDefaultConfig` → `PmRolesConfig`\n- Live priority-order validation example (`pm_priority_live.rs`) for manual testing against real providers",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2442,
      "deletions": 1098,
      "changed_files": 63
    },
    {
      "number": 587,
      "title": "agent-embedding: terminal sessions, attention lifecycle, wave workspace UI",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo build && cargo test\nuv run pytest python/tests/\n# In Concerto: select a wave → Work tab shows embedded Ghostty terminal\n# Attention queue shows Interactive / Algedonic items with resolve API\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nShips the core agent-embedding wave: terminal sessions tracked by `lfd`, attention queue completion with HTTP create/resolve, and a wave workspace UI in Concerto with embedded Ghostty terminals. The daemon now persists `terminal_sessions` in the store, emits lifecycle events, and Concerto consumes them as a tabbed terminal workspace keyed by session ID.\n\nAttention kinds collapse from four to two (Interactive / Algedonic). The HTTP API gains `POST /attention` and `POST /attention/:id/resolve` so `lf` CLI can create and resolve attention items. Reconciliation logic simplifies: queue blocks require manual resolution, step failures auto-resolve when superseded, interactive items resolve when the wave restarts.\n\nWave lifecycle is now fully managed from Concerto — create, configure, run, stop, land, next, restart-step, clone, delete, and triggers all work through `WaveDetailPanel` and `StepRunner`. The Python client gains `status` support for wave creation.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Terminal sessions** (`TerminalSession` type, migration 032, store CRUD, HTTP routes, `TerminalWorkspaceStore` in Swift) — daemon-persisted coding sessions with launch/complete/cleanup lifecycle\n- **Attention API** — HTTP create/resolve endpoints, simplified reconciliation, collapsed attention kinds\n- **Wave workspace UI** — `TerminalWorkspaceView` with embedded Ghostty tabs, `WaveWorkspaceView` routing, `WaveDetailPanel` lifecycle controls\n- **Bundled daemon** — `LFD_PARENT_PID` parent-watch for clean shutdown, `LFD_DISABLE_WORKTREE_JANITOR` flag, stabilized handoff from `BundledDaemonManager`\n- **CI** — skip `ConcertoUITests` in CI, add timeout\n- **Roadmap** — updated agent-embedding strategy and added `wave/lfd/` design docs (tmux study, daemon-aware CLI, daemon-hosted shells)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 5385,
      "deletions": 1097,
      "changed_files": 84
    },
    {
      "number": 588,
      "title": "agent-embedding: terminal multiplexer, attention lifecycle, flow engine extensions",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# Terminal workspace with multiplexer splits\n# Select a wave in Concerto → Work tab shows embedded Ghostty terminals in a binary split tree\n\n# Attention queue with simplified kinds\ncurl localhost:2486/api/attention              # Interactive + Algedonic only\ncurl -X POST localhost:2486/api/attention       # create items via HTTP\ncurl -X POST localhost:2486/api/attention/:id/resolve\n\n# Flow engine: xor, or, loop constructs\nlf flow run build                              # new build/ flow family\nlf flow run garden                             # renamed from tend\n\n# Terminal sessions persisted in lfd\ncurl localhost:2486/api/terminal-sessions\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis branch ships the core agent-embedding wave: Concerto becomes a terminal-native workspace rather than a chat client. The main surfaces are an attention queue for human checkpoints, embedded Ghostty terminals in a per-wave multiplexer layout, and wave lifecycle management — all backed by `lfd` persistent state rather than Swift-only models.\n\nThe flow engine gains `xor` (exclusive routing), `or` (multi-select routing), and `loop` constructs. Built-in flows are reorganized: `tend` → `garden`, new `build/` family, and VSM governance flows for systems 2–5. The `lfd` store adds `terminal_sessions` with full CRUD and SSE event streaming.\n\n## Changes\n\n**Concerto (Swift)**\n- `MultiplexerView` + `MultiplexerStore`: recursive binary split tree per wave with four pane types (terminal, markdown, diff, launchpad), persisted via UserDefaults\n- `KeyboardRouter` + `ShortcutAction`: focus-aware dispatch — typing goes to focused tmux pane, shortcuts stay in SwiftUI\n- `TmuxSession` + `TmuxSessionRegistry`: tmux-backed terminal sessions with cleanup on wave switch\n- `GhosttyTerminalView` rewritten for direct key event input with IME detection\n- `TerminalWorkspaceStore` manages session ordering and selection per repo\n- Attention queue UI simplified to Interactive/Algedonic kinds, removing unused variants\n- New test suites: multiplexer, keyboard router, Ghostty terminal view, tmux registry\n\n**Daemon (Rust lfd)**\n- `terminal_sessions` table (migration 032) with create/get/list/update/end routes\n- Attention collapsed from 5+ kinds to `InteractiveStep` and `Algedonic` with HTTP create/resolve API\n- Wave executor extended for terminal session lifecycle and wave-scoped workspace routing\n- Terminal session events integrated into the SSE event stream\n\n**Flow engine (Rust)**\n- `Xor`, `Or`, `Loop` flow items with concrete expansion and action dispatch\n- Built-in flows reorganized: `build/`, `garden/`, `vsm/`, `algedonic/`\n- VSM governance steps for systems 2–5 (scan + assess per system)\n- `tend` steps renamed to `garden` (assess, scan)\n\n**Wave roadmap**\n- Shipped items (attention queue, terminal embedding, workspace context) deleted\n- Remaining items renumbered: beat synthesizer, wave lifecycle UI, portfolio view, calibration view, window composition\n- Agent-embedding README updated with execution model, multiplexer design, and transport boundary guidance\n\n**Other**\n- PM module: Linear priority scoring with configurable weights, Asana cleanup\n- Chord model wave: `tend` → `garden` rename, VSM chord configs, worker pools, concurrent ingest items\n- `lf ops pr` and `lf commit` workflow improvements",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3625,
      "deletions": 1776,
      "changed_files": 52
    },
    {
      "number": 589,
      "title": "pm: add export, push-diff, and per-wave bootstrap",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op pm init pm           # bootstrap one wave's PM project\nlf op pm init --all        # bootstrap every wave/ project\nlf op pm pull pm           # rewrite wave from PM (remote wins)\nlf op pm export pm         # push wave to PM (local wins)\nlf op pm push-diff pm      # push only branch-changed items\nlf op pm status            # show linked waves and counts\n```\n\nAll subcommands accept `<wave>`, `-w <wave>`, or `--all`.\n\n## Summary\n\nExpands the PM sync surface from pull-only to a full bidirectional workflow. `export` pushes the complete local wave state to the PM provider (local wins). `push-diff` uses git to detect which wave items changed on the current branch and pushes only those — this is what `deploy` now calls instead of the old blanket `pm sync`.\n\n`init` is narrowed from \"bootstrap all waves at once\" to a per-wave command (with `--all` opt-in), and now writes `linear_id`/`asana_id` frontmatter into item files so future pulls and pushes can match by ID.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `pm_export` and `pm_push_diff` ops with provider-level create/update logic for Linear, Asana, and Notion\n- `pm_init` refactored to per-wave with `PmInitOptions`; links existing remote items by name and writes PM IDs into frontmatter\n- PM trait (`PmProvider`) gains `list_projects`, `priority_field`, and richer `update_item` with partial updates\n- Flows updated: `deploy` calls `pm push-diff`, `sync` calls `pm pull`, `build-or-silent` and `garden-or-silent` pull PM state before starting\n- Garden and plan flows (`wave-reduce`, `wave-polish`, `wave-expand`) now end with `deploy` to land and sync automatically\n- Default PM provider switched from Notion to Linear in config\n- New `diff_names_under` and `log_grep` git helpers for scoping diffs and finding commits\n- Wave items across agent-embedding, lfd, pm, chord-model, concerto updated with PM IDs and content from Linear sync\n- CLI parser tests for new subcommand shapes",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2576,
      "deletions": 1005,
      "changed_files": 59
    },
    {
      "number": 590,
      "title": "pm: priority buckets, notion prep, and init rework",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow\nlf ops pm init          # bootstrap fresh PM projects for all waves\nlf ops pm pull pm       # rewrite one wave from PM; remote wins\nlf ops pm pull --all    # rewrite every wave from PM\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the numbered-stage roadmap model (`01-*`, `02-*`, ...) with semantic priority buckets (`1-*` Urgent through `4-*` Low) across the entire system: wave docs, agent prompts, ingest logic, PM sync, and provider adapters.\n\nReworks `pm init` to bootstrap all waves at once with a fresh team and per-wave projects, replacing the old single-wave `--wave` / `--all` interface. Adds `pm pull` as the new remote-wins rewrite command. Introduces `PriorityBucket` as the shared type flowing through Asana and Linear adapters, so items carry semantic priority instead of numeric rank.\n\nWave item files across the repo are renamed from legacy numbered prefixes to the new bucket scheme.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `PriorityBucket` enum (Urgent/High/Medium/Low) in `lfd::pm` replaces `rank: u32` on `PmItem`, `PmItemCreate`, `PmItemUpdate`\n- `pm init` creates a team and projects for every wave in one shot; removes `--wave` flag\n- `pm pull` added as the remote-wins import (replaces `pm import`)\n- `pm export` CLI command removed; export is now internal to sync\n- Asana/Linear adapters gain `create_team`, `find_team`, `create_project_in_team`, and priority mapping\n- Agent prompts (`ingest`, `update-wave`, `design`, `scan`, `split-wave`) rewritten for bucket model\n- `docs/wave-authoring.md` updated end-to-end\n- All `wave/*/` item files renamed from `0N-*` to bucket prefixes\n- Test in `builtins.rs` asserts prompt/doc parity with the new model",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2142,
      "deletions": 1039,
      "changed_files": 64
    },
    {
      "number": 591,
      "title": "lfd: observe wave CLI runs through runtime journals",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nRun any `lf` command in a wave worktree — the CLI now writes structured events to `.lf/journal/runs/<run-id>/events.jsonl`:\n\n```bash\n# in a wave worktree\nlf build\ncat .lf/journal/runs/*/events.jsonl\n```\n\nThe daemon picks up journal events automatically via the event hub.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds a runtime journal so the `lfd` daemon can observe what the CLI is doing in wave worktrees without requiring the CLI to talk to the daemon directly. Each `lf` invocation writes a JSONL event stream covering run, flow, and step lifecycle (started/completed/errored/escalated). The journal is scoped to wave worktrees only — runs in the main repo are silently ignored. The daemon spawns a journal watcher that reads these events and publishes them through the existing event hub.\n\nThis is the first half of the daemon-aware CLI contract: the CLI writes, the daemon reads. No socket, no coordination — just the filesystem.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `journal` module with `emit()`, `read_events()`, and thread-local run context that scopes events to a single CLI invocation\n- Journal files are excluded from git via worktree-local `info/exclude` (not `.gitignore` in the main repo)\n- `lf.rs` wraps all command paths in `with_runtime` / `in_repo_runtime` to emit run-level events\n- Flow runner emits flow and step start/complete/error events at each boundary\n- New `lfd::journal` watcher spawned alongside other daemon tasks\n- New `LfdEvent` variants for journal observations in the daemon event system\n- `TestRepo::create_wave_worktree` helper for journal tests\n- Wave design docs updated: shipped journal contract into item 01, added daemon-hosted shells as item 02",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1431,
      "deletions": 67,
      "changed_files": 13
    },
    {
      "number": 592,
      "title": "concerto: wave workspaces with terminal-native keyboard routing",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nswift test --package-path swift\ncargo test --all\n./dev run-debug\n```\n\nIn Concerto:\n- Focus an embedded terminal pane — typing flows character-by-character through Ghostty's native key events\n- Split panes with `⌃⇧5` (vertical) and `⌃⇧'` (horizontal); close with `⌘W`, navigate with `⌥⌘←/→`\n- Open command palette — pane actions show shortcuts pulled from the keyboard router, so labels can't drift\n\n## Summary\n\nPolishes Concerto's workspace layer around terminal-native input and wave-scoped workspaces. Split bindings move from `⌘\\` / `⇧⌘\\` to keycode-based `⌃⇧5` / `⌃⇧'` so they work reliably in terminal mode without conflicting with shell input. The command palette now sources shortcut labels from `KeyboardRouter`, scores fuzzy matches by quality (prefix > word-start > scattered), and exposes split/close/new-shell actions when a multiplexer is active. `MultiplexerLayout` and `MultiplexerStore` are simplified with a shared `replacingLeaf` helper and cleaner workspace initialization. Shipped and obsolete wave items under `wave/` are cleaned up.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Keycode-based split shortcuts (`⌃⇧5`, `⌃⇧'`) replace character-based `⌘\\` bindings\n- `KeyboardRouter.normalizeKey` handles `fiveKeyCode` and `quoteKeyCode` for terminal mode\n- Command palette reads shortcut labels from `KeyboardRouter.keyDisplay(for:)` and adds multiplexer actions\n- Fuzzy matching upgraded to scored ranking (prefix > word-boundary > subsequence)\n- `LayoutNode` deduplicates recursive tree walks via `replacingLeaf(_:with:)`\n- `MultiplexerStore` and `TerminalWorkspaceStore` simplified with fewer intermediate state copies\n- Disable automatic window tabbing (`NSWindow.allowsAutomaticWindowTabbing = false`)\n- Delete shipped wave items and obsolete `wave/lfd/` design docs",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 243,
      "deletions": 923,
      "changed_files": 22
    },
    {
      "number": 593,
      "title": "chord model: worker capacity, xor/loop engine, ops→op rename",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf op pr                    # renamed from lf ops pr\nlf op wt create my-feature  # all ops subcommands are now lf op\nlf op land\n```\n\nWave configs now use `workers: N` instead of `serialized: bool`:\n\n```yaml\nworkers: 3   # max concurrent runs (replaces serialized: true/false)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThree threads woven together in the chord-model wave:\n\n1. **`lf ops` → `lf op`** — renamed across CLI, docs, builtins, and tests. Shorter, consistent with `lf op <verb>` grammar.\n\n2. **Worker capacity model** — replaces the binary `serialized` flag with `workers: u32`, letting waves run N concurrent runs. Activations that exceed capacity are enqueued and dispatched when slots free up. Includes a new migration (`033_wave_workers`), capacity-aware `dispatch_or_enqueue_activation`, and pause-on-conflict handling for manual resolution errors.\n\n3. **Flow engine: xor, or, and loop** — adds `Xor` (exclusive routing), `Or` (multi-select, structurally aliased to Xor), and `Loop` (iterative with exit condition) as first-class `FlowItem` variants. VSM governance flows and garden-based tending use these new constructs.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `Wave.serialized: bool` → `Wave.workers: u32` with DB migration and serde default\n- `dispatch_or_enqueue_activation` checks active run count against wave capacity\n- `FlowItem::Xor`, `FlowItem::Loop` added; `OrDef` becomes a type alias for `XorDef`\n- `docs/lfops.md` renamed to `docs/lfop.md`; all references updated\n- `lfd`: rename linked-worktree branches from their owning worktree\n- `lf land`: stages uncommitted changes and clears `scratch/`\n- Wave chord-model items updated to reflect shipped step/flow names\n- Worker-pools design doc folded into chord-model items (shipped)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 759,
      "deletions": 704,
      "changed_files": 98
    },
    {
      "number": 594,
      "title": "agent-embedding: interactive checkpoints, wave workspaces, pm bootstrap, and garden rename",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# interactive checkpoints surface in the attention queue\nlf flow run build     # interactive steps (review-design, wave/review) now create AttentionItems\n\n# pm bootstrap\nlf pm init            # connect Notion/Asana/Linear providers\nlf pm pull            # sync items with priority buckets\nlf pm status          # view provider health\n\n# concerto\n# wave workspace panes with terminal-native keyboard routing\n# multiplexer layout persistence and wave detail in panes\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a wave hits an interactive step (design review, wave review), the executor now creates an `AttentionItem` with context pulled from the worktree — design doc path, mutation summary, first meaningful line as summary. Completing the terminal session auto-resolves the attention item. This closes the loop between headless agent runs and the human-attention model that Concerto's queue is built on.\n\nAlongside that, several supporting systems land:\n\n- **Concerto workspaces**: `MultiplexerView` rewritten with persistent layout trees, wave detail embedded in panes, terminal-native keyboard routing (`KeyboardRouter`), and a `TerminalWorkspaceStore` for session lifecycle\n- **PM bootstrap**: `lf pm init/pull/status` CLI, Notion provider with OAuth + page block parsing, Asana and Linear enriched with priority buckets and richer sync\n- **Runtime journals**: `lfd` observes wave CLI runs through structured journal entries\n- **Chord model**: `tend` renamed to `garden` throughout flows, steps, and docs; VSM governance flows added; `xor`/`or`/`loop` flow engine routing\n- **Wave roadmap**: agent-embedding items renumbered and updated for shipped work; beat synthesizer retired\n\n## Changes\n\n- `WaveExecutor`: creates interactive `AttentionItem` on wait, resolves on terminal session completion\n- `find_attention_item_for_run` added to store trait (sqlite + postgres)\n- `AttentionItem` model gains `interactive` kind with step-scoped context (design path, mutation summary, terminal session ID)\n- `MultiplexerView`, `MultiplexerStore`, `MultiplexerLayout` rewritten for persistent pane trees\n- `TerminalWorkspaceView` + `TerminalWorkspaceStore` for embedded terminal session management\n- `KeyboardRouter` + `ShortcutAction` for native keyboard dispatch in workspaces\n- `lf pm` subcommand with `init`, `pull`, `status`; Notion OAuth + block API client\n- `journal` module for structured runtime observation\n- `terminal_sessions` HTTP routes for session CRUD\n- Flow engine: `tend` → `garden` rename, VSM system flows, `build-or-silent` / `garden-or-silent` routing\n- Tests for interactive attention lifecycle, multiplexer layout, workspace store, keyboard routing",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1940,
      "deletions": 609,
      "changed_files": 49
    },
    {
      "number": 595,
      "title": "pm/notion: add Notion provider with OAuth, page blocks, and bootstrap",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ops auth notion        # connect Notion with OAuth\nlf pm init my-wave        # bootstrap a Notion database for a wave\nlf pm pull my-wave        # sync items from Notion into local roadmap files\nlf pm status my-wave      # show sync status\n```\n\n```yaml\n# .lf/config.yaml\npm:\n  provider: notion\nnotion:\n  parent_page: 32af8f99-...  # optional: target a specific parent page\n  title_property: Name\n  status_property: Status\n  done_value: Done\n  priority_property: Priority\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds Notion as a first-class PM provider alongside Asana and Linear. The Notion client implements the full `PmProvider` trait — creating databases, syncing items with priority buckets, and converting between Notion block trees and markdown. OAuth uses the same PKCE + local callback flow as Asana.\n\nThe PM trait itself grew to support provider bootstrapping (`create_team`, `find_team`, `create_project_in_team`) so `lf pm init` can set up a wave's Notion database from scratch. Priority tracking moved from a numeric `rank` field to typed `PriorityBucket` variants (urgent/high/medium/low).\n\n## Changes\n\n- `notion.rs` — full Notion API client: databases, pages, properties, rate-limit retry\n- `notion_blocks.rs` — bidirectional Notion block ↔ markdown conversion (headings, lists, code, callouts, toggles, tables)\n- `provider_auth.rs` — Notion OAuth flow with client-credentials token exchange\n- `config.rs` — `NotionConfig` struct for schema property overrides and parent page targeting\n- `ops/pm.rs` — `init` and `pull` operations, provider bootstrapping, refactored context building\n- CLI — `lf ops auth notion` / `lfq auth notion` commands, Notion in auth status table\n- Wave yamls updated to reflect completed Notion client milestone",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2441,
      "deletions": 202,
      "changed_files": 53
    },
    {
      "number": 596,
      "title": "lfd: shared flow execution engine with runtime journals",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# CLI runs now emit structured lifecycle events\nlf build\nls .lf/runtime/journal/\n\n# daemon observes CLI-initiated runs via journal polling\nlfd\n\n# flow engine handles xor routing and loops in both CLI and daemon\nlf <flow-with-xor-or-loop>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nExtracts flow execution into a shared `FlowEngine` with a `StepExecutor` trait, so both the CLI (`lf`) and daemon (`lfd`) use the same sequencing logic for steps, xor routing, loops, and fork/and. The CLI emits structured lifecycle events (started/completed/errored) into runtime journals, and the daemon polls those journals to observe CLI-initiated runs without requiring daemon-managed processes.\n\nThis is the foundation for the lfd execution model: one execution path, two executors. The daemon adds scheduling, supervision, and persistence on top. The CLI remains standalone and observable.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `engine::execution` module with `FlowEngine`, `StepExecutor` trait, cursor-based resumable execution, and xor/loop support\n- New `journal` module for structured lifecycle events (`LfNode`, `LfEventType`, `LfEventFields`) written to `.lf/runtime/journal/`\n- New `lfd::journal` poller that watches worktree journals and syncs run state into the daemon store\n- `lf` CLI emits step-level journal events during flow and step execution\n- Wave executor simplified: removed fork worktree execution (`fork.rs`), daemon now uses shared `FlowEngine` via `DaemonFlowExecutor`\n- Flow model expanded: `Xor` (exclusive-or branching) and `Loop` constructs added, `Or` renamed to `Xor` for single-select routing\n- New terminal session HTTP routes and types for daemon-hosted tmux sessions\n- Execution cursor persisted in store for resumable daemon runs\n- Wave roadmap: new `wave/lfd/` tracking daemon-CLI integration items",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1944,
      "deletions": 3737,
      "changed_files": 42
    },
    {
      "number": 597,
      "title": "agent-embedding: interactive checkpoints, multiplexer panes, activation dispatch",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf ops ingest --item 2-daemon-integrity.md   # target a specific roadmap item\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nShips three main pieces of the agent-embedding wave: interactive checkpoints that let wave runs pause for human review via terminal sessions, a refactored activation dispatch that separates enqueue-then-dispatch from spawn-immediate paths based on worker count, and Concerto multiplexer panes that surface wave detail inline.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Interactive checkpoints** — wave executor can pause a run at a terminal session, surface an attention item, wait for resolution, then resume execution. Includes test coverage for failed-session and context-building paths.\n- **Activation dispatch refactor** — `dispatch_or_enqueue_activation` split into `enqueue_pending_activation` + `dispatch_wave_if_ready` (single-worker) vs `spawn_immediate_activation` (multi-worker), making the concurrency model explicit.\n- **`lf ops ingest --item`** — new `-i/--item` flag to target a specific roadmap item instead of auto-picking by priority.\n- **Concerto UI** — wave detail moved into multiplexer panes, terminal color scheme updated from burgundy to slate grey, `WaveContentParser` added for richer content rendering, brand colors refreshed.\n- **Roadmap housekeeping** — canonical numbering across all wave items (stripped `01:`/`02:` title prefixes), deleted shipped and stale items, added PM sync design doc.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1441,
      "deletions": 488,
      "changed_files": 60
    },
    {
      "number": 598,
      "title": "lfd: shared execution engine, notion pm, garden flows, wave workspaces",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# shared flow execution — CLI and daemon use the same engine\nlf run build\nlfd  # daemon executes the same flows via runtime journals\n\n# notion pm provider\nlf pm init --provider notion\nlf pm pull\nlf pm push-diff\n\n# garden flows (formerly tend)\nlf run garden\nlf run garden-or-silent\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nUnifies flow execution between the CLI (`lf`) and daemon (`lfd`) through a shared execution engine backed by runtime journals. The daemon no longer has its own fork of flow execution — both paths go through `engine::execution`. Adds a Notion PM provider alongside the existing Asana and Linear integrations, with OAuth, page-block rendering, and per-wave bootstrap. Renames the `tend` flow area to `garden` and introduces VSM governance flows. On the Concerto side, ships terminal-native wave workspaces with multiplexer layout, tmux session management, and keyboard routing.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Shared execution engine** (`engine/execution.rs`, `lfd/journal.rs`): CLI and daemon share one code path; daemon observes runs through runtime journals\n- **Real CLI executor for lfd**: daemon can invoke `lf` as a subprocess, replacing the old tmux-only fork path\n- **Notion PM provider** (`lfd/pm/notion.rs`, `notion_blocks.rs`): OAuth auth, page/block CRUD, rich-text rendering, bootstrap from existing Notion pages\n- **PM improvements**: priority buckets, export command, push-diff for incremental sync, per-wave bootstrap\n- **Chord model**: `tend` → `garden` rename, VSM governance flows (S2–S5 scan/assess steps), xor/or/loop flow operators, worker capacity tracking\n- **Concerto wave workspaces**: `MultiplexerView`, `TerminalWorkspaceView`, tmux session registry, keyboard routing with chord shortcuts\n- **Attention lifecycle**: richer attention item model, interactive session checkpoints\n- **CI fix**: `disable_tmux` flag on `WaveExecutor` so test-injected `MockRunner` isn't bypassed when tmux is present in CI\n- **Docs**: `lfops` → `lfop` rename, updated wave authoring and getting-started guides",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 0,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 0
    },
    {
      "number": 599,
      "title": "redesign: restructure waves, simplify flows, add gardening",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf garden          # scan → assess → xor(act, silence)\nlf govern-operations   # ingest → xor(s1-build, silence)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nRestructures the wave directory and flow system around clearer naming, VSM alignment, and a gardening philosophy. Waves are reorganized from abstract groupings (agent-embedding, chord-model, concerto, dogfood, redesign) into purpose-driven areas (macos, ios, model, root). Flows are simplified — removing plan/ and scan/ flow categories, collapsing garden-or-silent into garden, and adding govern-operations as the S1 VSM layer.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Wave restructure**: renamed `agent-embedding` → `macos`, `chord-model` → `model`, `dogfood` → `ios`, `redesign` → `root`; dissolved `concerto` and `trust` waves, redistributing items\n- **Garden flow**: `garden` now owns the full scan → assess → xor cycle; `garden-act` extracts the mutate → review action path\n- **VSM flows**: added `govern-operations` (S1) with ingest → xor(s1-build, silence); reordered VSM table S1–S5\n- **New build flow**: `s1-build` — kickoff → code → deploy with no review gates\n- **Removed flows**: `qa-deploy`, `reorg`, `wave-reduce/polish/expand`, `scan`, `garden-or-silent`\n- **Removed steps**: `scan/scan-report`, `scan/scan-plan` and their builtin registrations\n- **PM auth**: consolidated OAuth-only auth and team auth into `2-provider-auth.md`\n- **README and flow docs**: updated to reflect all structural changes",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 202,
      "deletions": 935,
      "changed_files": 74
    },
    {
      "number": 600,
      "title": "concerto-ui: terminate hosted test app on CI",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nFixes CI hangs where `xcodebuild test` completes all tests successfully but never exits because the hosted test app keeps running.\n\nTwo complementary fixes:\n\n1. **Swift observer** — `HostedTestAppTerminationObserver` calls `NSApp.terminate` after the test bundle finishes, so the hosted app shuts down cleanly. Wrapped in `#if os(macOS)` to stay SwiftPM-compatible.\n\n2. **CI timeout resilience** — the CI harness now watches stdout for Swift Testing's `✔ Test run with N tests...` success summary. If xcodebuild still hangs after that line, the run is treated as a success instead of a timeout failure.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `HostedTestAppTerminationObserver.swift` using `XCTestObservation` to terminate the app post-tests\n- Whole-file `#if os(macOS)` gate so the observer compiles but is absent on non-macOS targets\n- CI script regex-matches the final success summary line as a fallback success signal\n- Timeout handling split into three cases: xcresult success, stdout success summary, or real failure",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 49,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 601,
      "title": "lfd: polish tmux attach helpers",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo fmt --check\ncargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings\ncargo test -p loopflow terminal_sessions -- --nocapture\nswift test --package-path swift --filter GhosttyTerminalViewTests\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPolish pass on the tmux attach helpers to make test fixtures more realistic and the Swift attach contract more explicit.\n\nThe Rust test helper now gates `tmux_name` on `TMUX_TERMINAL_SOURCE` so non-tmux sessions don't carry synthetic tmux metadata. On the Swift side, `TerminalAttachCommand` gains an explicit `env` property (defaulting to empty) and passes it through to `GhosttyTerminalView` instead of hardcoding `[:]` at the call site. Tests assert the env contract on both local and remote attach paths.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Rust: `make_terminal_session` only populates `tmux_name` for tmux-sourced sessions\n- Swift: thread `command.env` through `SessionTerminalSurface` instead of hardcoded empty dict\n- Swift: add `env` property to `TerminalAttachCommand` with empty default\n- Tests: assert `env.isEmpty` on both local and remote tmux attach commands\n- Scratch: add validation commands and review notes for the polish pass",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 10,
      "deletions": 5,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 603,
      "title": "lfd: terminal attach connection contract",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow terminal_sessions -- --nocapture\nswift test --package-path swift --filter GhosttyTerminalViewTests\nswift test --package-path swift --filter LocalWaveServiceAuthTests\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplace `TerminalLaunchSpecDto` with `TerminalConnectionInfo` so Concerto connects directly to tmux sessions. No terminal bytes flow through lfd — the attach endpoint returns session name, host, cwd, and status, and the client builds its own `tmux attach-session` (local) or `ssh -t host \"tmux attach-session\"` (remote).\n\n## Changes\n\n- Rust: new attach endpoint returns `TerminalConnectionInfo` with `session_name`, `host`, `cwd`, `status`\n- Migration 035 adds `tmux_name` column to terminal sessions\n- Swift: `TerminalWorkspaceView` builds tmux commands from connection info\n- Tests: Rust attach tests verify response shape; Swift tests verify local and remote command construction\n- Wave lfd: restructured items, removed shipped architecture studies",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 525,
      "deletions": 920,
      "changed_files": 29
    },
    {
      "number": 604,
      "title": "wave: re-add interactive checkpoint test for terminal session resolution",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nRe-introduces the interactive checkpoint test that was removed during an earlier rebase when per-step execution was replaced with flow-level tmux sessions. The new test validates the full lifecycle: a completed terminal session resolves its associated interactive attention item.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add `completed_terminal_session_resolves_interactive_attention` test covering the path from a succeeded terminal session through `wait_for_terminal_session_and_resume` to attention item resolution\n- Import `AttentionItem` in test module to support the new test setup\n- Remove placeholder comment about removed tests",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 118,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 607,
      "title": "ingest: auto-refresh PM-backed waves before picking",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ingest -- --nocapture\ncargo test -p loopflow --test golden_prompt -- --nocapture\ncargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings\n```\n\nTo verify the user-facing path, configure a PM-backed wave and run:\n\n```bash\nlf ops ingest --wave <wave-name>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`lf ops ingest` now calls `pm_pull` to refresh the local wave mirror from the PM provider before selecting an item — so reprioritized, added, or deleted remote items are picked up automatically. If the pull fails (provider unreachable, auth issues), ingest emits a warning and falls back to the existing local `wave/<name>/` files. Waves without a `pm` block are unaffected.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `ops/ingest.rs`: call `pm_pull` when `wave_pm_is_enabled`, warn on failure\n- `Progress` trait: add `warning()` with default impl delegating to `error()`\n- `CliProgress` and `TracingProgress`: implement `warning()`\n- Two new tests: successful PM refresh changes pick order; failed pull warns and continues\n- Docs updated: README, wave-authoring, ingest step prompt, wave/pm README\n- Ship `wave/pm/1-ingest-auto-import.md` (moved to scratch, removed from backlog)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 201,
      "deletions": 54,
      "changed_files": 9
    },
    {
      "number": 608,
      "title": "concerto: cache transcript state, add Ghostty workspace shells",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nswift test --package-path swift\ncd swift && xcodegen generate && xcodebuild build -project LoopflowSwift.xcodeproj -scheme Concerto -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n```\n\nLaunch Concerto, select a local wave workspace:\n\n- **Open Terminal** opens an external Ghostty window attached to `lf-<waveId>-shell`\n- **Open Internally** selects the same tmux-backed shell in Concerto's embedded Ghostty surface\n- Stream an assistant response — transcript updates without view-level regrouping or timestamp rescans\n\n## Summary\n\nMoves transcript hot-path derivation (`groupedTranscript`, `latestAssistantMessageId`, `timestampLabels`) out of view computed properties and into `SessionState`, so streaming deltas update derived state incrementally instead of recomputing over the full transcript each frame. Adds a `MessageSegmentCache` in `MessageRow` keyed on content length to skip re-parsing markdown segments on every delta. Scroll animation now respects `reduceMotion`, and `StreamingCursorView` gets a stable identity to prevent flicker.\n\nOn the terminal side, Ghostty becomes the default external terminal (`TerminalApp.defaultExternal`). A new `WorkspaceShell` model ties each wave to a persistent tmux session (`lf-<waveId>-shell`) that can be opened externally in Ghostty or internally in the embedded terminal surface. The inline name-editing shortcut (`E`) and related `editWaveName` notification are removed.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `SessionState`: new `groupedTranscript`, `latestAssistantMessageId`, `timestampLabels` properties maintained incrementally via `appendTranscriptEntry` / `updateTranscriptEntry`; reverse index (`transcriptIndexById`) replaces `firstIndex(where:)` lookups\n- `TranscriptGroup` promoted to public type in `LoopflowCore`\n- `MessageRow`: `MessageSegmentCache` avoids re-parsing on each streaming delta; `StreamingCursorView` keyed by message id\n- `WaveSessionView`: reads derived state from `SessionState` instead of computing locally; scroll uses `DesignAnimation.fast(reduceMotion)`\n- `TerminalLauncher`: new `launchGhostty` method; hardcoded `.warp` references replaced with `.defaultExternal`\n- `TerminalWorkspaceView`: `WorkspaceShell` manages per-wave tmux sessions; \"Open Terminal\" / \"Open Internally\" buttons; `user_shell` sessions attach directly without server round-trip\n- `WaveDetailPanel`, `WaveRow`: inline name-editing removed\n- `WaveStore`: preserves sidebar order across server refreshes\n- `AppPreferences`: adds `defaultTerminal` setting\n- New wave planning docs under `wave/chatgui/` and `wave/chattui/`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 851,
      "deletions": 334,
      "changed_files": 31
    },
    {
      "number": 609,
      "title": "wave crons: supplementary scheduled flows per wave",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```yaml\n# member wave — workers handle primary flow, crons sweep maintenance\nflow: build\nworkers: 2\nmode: loop\ncrons:\n  - flow: wave-polish\n    schedule: \"0 0 * * 1\"\n  - flow: wave-reduce\n    schedule: \"0 0 1 * *\"\n\n# root wave — no workers, all work from crons\nflow: garden\nworkers: 0\nmode: manual\ncrons:\n  - flow: govern-identity\n    schedule: \"0 0 * * 0\"\n```\n\n```python\nimport loopflow.api as loopflow\n\nloopflow.create_wave(\"mywave\", repo=\".\", flow=\"build\",\n    crons=[{\"flow\": \"wave-polish\", \"schedule\": \"0 0 * * 1\"}])\nloopflow.update_wave(\"mywave\",\n    crons=[{\"flow\": \"wave-polish\", \"schedule\": \"0 0 * * 1\"}])\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWaves can now have a list of cron-scheduled supplementary flows alongside their primary flow. Crons fire independently of the worker pool — `workers: 0` is valid for waves that only run from cron schedules. The old `WaveMode::Cron` variant is removed; `mode` is now strictly `loop` or `manual`, and scheduled work lives in the `crons` list.\n\nA DB migration (`036_wave_crons.sql`) creates a `wave_crons` table and backfills any existing `mode: cron` waves into the new shape (converting them to `mode: manual, workers: 0` with a cron entry).\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `WaveCron` type across Rust, Python, and Swift models\n- `wave_crons` table with CRUD operations on both SQLite and Postgres stores\n- Cron trigger loop rewritten to iterate `wave_crons` rows instead of filtering waves by mode\n- `workers` field no longer floors to 1 — `workers: 0` means \"cron-only, no primary dispatch\"\n- `force_parallel: true` on cron activations so they bypass worker capacity checks\n- HTTP routes: `POST /waves` and `PATCH /waves/:id` accept `crons`, new `GET /waves/:id/crons` endpoint\n- Python client passes `crons` through `create_wave` and `update_wave`\n- Docs and wave model updated; `3-wave-scheduling.md` outlines the next step toward `loops`/`crons` as the sole scheduling primitives",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1045,
      "deletions": 311,
      "changed_files": 49
    },
    {
      "number": 610,
      "title": "lf: commit dirty files after standalone step runs",
      "body": "## Summary\n- Standalone steps (`lf gate`, `lf implement`, etc.) didn't commit uncommitted files after the agent finished\n- The flow executor called `commit_step_work` after each step, but the standalone `run_target` path skipped it\n- Added `commit_workflow(add: true)` after standalone step success, matching flow executor behavior\n\n## Test plan\n- [ ] Run `lf gate` on a branch with uncommitted files — verify they're committed after the step\n- [ ] Run a flow (`lf build`) — verify no double-commit (second commit_workflow no-ops)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 11,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
