    {
      "number": 612,
      "title": "pm: asana rich text via html_notes",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow asana_html\ncargo test -p loopflow asana\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAsana task descriptions now round-trip as markdown instead of plaintext. The Asana client reads `html_notes` (falling back to `notes` for older tasks) and writes `html_notes` on create and update. A new `asana_html` module handles bidirectional markdown ↔ Asana HTML conversion, supporting headings, lists (nested), bold, italic, code, strikethrough, links, and horizontal rules.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `rust/loopflow/src/lfd/pm/asana_html.rs` — markdown parser and Asana HTML parser with round-trip conversion\n- `AsanaClient` reads `html_notes` field, prefers it over `notes`, converts to markdown via `asana_html_to_markdown`\n- `AsanaClient` create/update writes `html_notes` instead of `notes`, converts from markdown via `markdown_to_asana_html`\n- Removed `2-asana-rich-text.md` wave item (shipped)\n- Bootstrapped wave items across ios, lfd, macos, model, old, redesign, root with PM metadata\n- Default PM provider switched from linear to asana in `.lf/config.yaml`\n- Cleanup: removed unused `Clone` derives, phantom marker on `UpdateTaskRequest`, and `task_path` helper",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1469,
      "deletions": 96,
      "changed_files": 52
    },
    {
      "number": 613,
      "title": "concerto: roadmap list/detail redesign + pm/ingest backend",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nswift test --package-path swift\ncargo test\nuv run python scripts/concerto-dev.py run-debug\n```\n\nOpen a wave with roadmap items — the workspace now opens into a compact list (left) + detail pane (right) + terminal. Select items with `j`/`k`/arrows/click, press `Return` or the detail pane's \"Ingest & build\" button to run.\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the single expandable-card roadmap pane with a list/detail split. The list shows compact selectable rows (title + priority, shipped items dimmed and pinned to the bottom), and the detail pane renders full markdown with an always-visible action button. Cross-pane selection flows through a shared `RoadmapSelection` environment object. Sidebar taglines now parse from the README's opening paragraph instead of requiring a `## Vision` section.\n\nOn the backend, Asana rich text ingestion moves to `html_notes` for full-fidelity rendering, the PM layer gains concurrent ingest coordination via claim-based locking, and wave roadmap docs get reorganized (shipped items archived to `wave/old/`, new items added across macos/model/pm/lfd waves).\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Concerto UI**: new `RoadmapPaneView` (list), `RoadmapDetailPaneView`, `RoadmapRowView`; `RoadmapSelection` shared state; keyboard nav (`j`/`k`/arrows/`Return`); shipped-last sort order; default layout now roadmap list (32%) + detail/terminal vertical split\n- **WaveContentParser**: `extractLeadingParagraph` for tagline extraction from README opening text, falling back to `## Vision`\n- **Rust PM**: `asana_html.rs` — full Asana HTML-to-markdown converter; Linear/Notion providers gain concurrent ingest support\n- **Rust ingest**: claim-based coordination for concurrent item ingestion across PM providers\n- **Wave docs**: terminal-tabs design doc; priority/status updates across waves; archived shipped items to `wave/old/`\n- **Tests**: `RoadmapOrderingTests` (shipped-last sort), `WaveContentParserTests` (tagline extraction)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 621,
      "deletions": 135,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 614,
      "title": "wave: concurrent ingest coordination via PM claim",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test ops::ingest::tests::ingest_prefers_pm_claimed_item\ncargo test lfd::pm::asana::tests::claim_item_verifies_assignee_after_assignment\ncargo test lfd::pm::linear::tests::claim_item_verifies_assignee_matches_viewer\ncargo test lfd::pm::notion::tests::claim_item_marks_page_in_progress\ncargo test lfd::pm::mod::tests::roadmap_item_frontmatter_mark_in_progress\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMultiple workers calling `ingest` no longer double-pick items. The PM provider arbitrates: each worker tries `pm_try_claim` before selecting an item, and providers verify the assignment stuck before proceeding. Frontmatter now carries `priority`, `rank`, `status`, `claimed_by`, and `claimed_at` as local cache of the coordination state.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Linear / Asana**: `claim_item` assigns to self then verifies the response confirms our identity; returns an error if another worker won the race\n- **Notion**: best-effort claim via \"In Progress\" status — no claimant identity, duplicates caught at PR time\n- **Ingest pipeline**: `try_claim_pm_item` runs before item selection; claimed item takes priority over local ordering; `write_claim_status` stamps frontmatter after the file moves to scratch/\n- **Wave item ordering**: frontmatter `priority` + `rank` now governs sort order, interleaving cleanly with legacy bucket/stage prefixes\n- **Priority sync**: `pm_pull` writes `priority`/`rank` into frontmatter; Notion reads priority from its select property and sorts by rank\n- **Kickoff step**: adds a de-risking phase — research risks before designing, with a structured findings table\n- **SQLite**: collapsed duplicate `read_wave_crons` helper\n- **Wave model docs**: folded shipped concurrent-ingest design into scheduling item, trimmed vsm-flow to reflect what's actually built vs. remaining",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 662,
      "deletions": 145,
      "changed_files": 11
    },
    {
      "number": 615,
      "title": "wave: mobile/relay/activity-normalization/stream-cursoring design docs",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nFour new wave design docs for mobile, relay infrastructure, activity normalization, and stream cursoring.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`wave/lfd/03-activity-normalization.md`** — typed activity events (shell, read, edit, write, search) normalized across providers, persisted and broadcast over WebSocket\n- **`wave/lfd/04-stream-cursoring.md`** — sequence numbers on WebSocket events, reconnect-with-cursor, ring buffer replay for mobile resilience\n- **`wave/ios/2-concerto-mobile.md`** — iOS app consuming LoopflowCore: wave list, wave detail, attention queue, QR pairing\n- **`wave/ios/2-studio-relay.md`** — WebSocket relay through loopflow.studio so phones on cellular can reach lfd on a workstation",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 237,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 616,
      "title": "Add gstack workstyle import and namespaced flows",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo run -q -p loopflow --bin lf -- --list\ncargo run -q -p loopflow --bin lf -- op gstack list\nuv run pytest python/tests/test_workstyle_convert.py -q\ncargo test -p loopflow --test discovery_tests -- --nocapture\ncargo test -p loopflow --test flow_tests -- --nocapture\n```\n\nYou should see gstack flows (`gstack-sprint`, `gstack-review`, `gstack-plan-manual`), 37 imported `gstack:*` steps, and passing converter/discovery tests.\n\n## Intent\n\nBring Garry Tan's gstack workflow into loopflow as an imported workstyle. The change adds generated gstack steps, namespaced flow/step discovery, sync/list/diff ops, and starter wave docs so teams can run gstack-style sprint and review flows without copying prompts by hand.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Imported workstyles live under `.lf/steps/<prefix>/` and are invoked as `<prefix>:<step>`.\n- Gstack sync can rely on `git`, network access to `garrytan/gstack`, and `uv run python -m loopflow.workstyle.convert`.\n- Reviewers care most about converter behavior and resolution semantics. The imported prompt bodies are generated artifacts.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Strip upstream telemetry, preamble wrappers, and Claude-specific integration sections during conversion.\n- Preserve colon syntax for steps and hyphenated names for flows so `lf --list` stays readable.\n- Use the built-in `synthesize` step for `gstack-review`; there is no generated `gstack:review-synthesize` step.\n- Clean built-in direction text to avoid identity framing while preserving the source intent.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Scheduled upstream sync.\n- Full native rewrite of every imported gstack prompt.\n- Backward compatibility for alternate upstream skill formats not covered by the converter tests.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 23249,
      "deletions": 105,
      "changed_files": 76
    },
    {
      "number": 617,
      "title": "prompt: split system/content sections for Claude plan compatibility",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nSplits the prompt assembly into system-safe and user-content sections. Loopflow instructions, RLM, voice, and surface stay in the system prompt. Repo content — docs, diffs, wave context, clipboard — moves to the task prompt (user message).\n\nThis avoids triggering third-party app classifiers that flag repo content in system prompts, and improves compatibility with Claude's plan mode.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `format_system_sections()` and `format_content_sections()` extract the two halves from the old monolithic `format_reference_sections()`\n- New `format_claude_system_prompt()` / `format_claude_task_prompt()` replace direct use of `format_context_prompt` / `format_task_prompt` in `launch.rs`\n- Summaries and diffs stay in the full prompt log but are excluded from the task prompt sent to Claude\n- Trimmed LOOPFLOW.md: removed Area, Chaining, and Quality sections that duplicated guidance already present in steps and directions\n- Trimmed README.md: removed Integrations and Requirements sections\n- Golden test files updated to reflect the leaner LOOPFLOW.md\n- `lf land` now clears `scratch/`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 113,
      "deletions": 474,
      "changed_files": 13
    },
    {
      "number": 618,
      "title": "pm: make pm auth oauth-only and branch-lock claims",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op auth asana\nlf op auth linear\nlf op auth configure asana   # exits: Asana requires OAuth. Run 'lf op auth asana' to connect.\nlf op auth configure linear  # exits: Linear requires OAuth. Run 'lf op auth linear' to connect.\nlf op auth configure notion  # exits: Notion requires OAuth. Run 'lf op auth notion' to connect.\n\nlf op pm init pm             # attaches/creates Asana's shared Working branch custom field\nlf ops ingest --wave pm      # Linear/Asana claims verify the current branch as the worker lock\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPM auth now has one supported setup path: OAuth. The old Asana and Linear env-var / API-key configure paths were bootstrap residue and pointed users at the wrong command on failure. Claim coordination also now uses each worker's branch name as the discriminator, so two workers sharing one PM account no longer both win simply because they share an assignee.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `lf op auth configure {asana,linear,notion}` and the matching HTTP route now exit with a clear OAuth-redirect error instead of storing a token.\n- `lfq auth linear` / `lf op auth linear` switch from API-key prompt to OAuth, matching Asana and Notion.\n- API-key configure support is retained only for Claude, Codex, and OpenCode Zen.\n- New `PmProvider::init_project`; Asana implementation ensures a workspace-level `Working branch` text custom field exists and is attached to the project.\n- Asana `claim_item` writes the branch into the `Working branch` custom field, re-reads, and rejects the claim if another worker overwrote it.\n- Linear `claim_item` similarly verifies branch ownership before declaring success.\n- Removed the PAT-based `scripts/setup-asana.py` helper and refreshed README / getting-started docs.\n- Tests cover the new OAuth gate in the CLI and the Asana init/claim flows.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Notion claim locking remains best-effort and arbitrated at PR time.\n- No migration for stale PM API-key rows already in the credential store; entrypoints are simply removed.\n- No default OAuth client credentials or secret-injection automation.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 632,
      "deletions": 237,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 619,
      "title": "lfd: accept Codex session input over HTTP",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow --test session_input_round_trip\n```\n\nThe test starts a live `lfd` HTTP router with fake Codex/Claude harnesses, creates a Codex session through `/v0/sessions`, posts input through `/v0/sessions/{id}/input`, observes the running turn through SSE, reconnects with `after_seq`, and verifies Claude input is rejected with a clear unsupported-harness error.\n\nOther checks run during gate:\n\n```bash\ncargo fmt --check\ncargo test -p loopflow --test dto_fixtures\ncargo test -p loopflow session_input\nuv run pytest python/tests/test_dto_fixtures.py python/tests/test_client.py python/tests/test_models.py -q\nswift test --package-path swift\ncargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings\n```\n\n## Intent\n\nLet a second device continue a running agent conversation without a terminal. Reading stays on the existing session SSE stream; writing is one authenticated `/v0/sessions/{id}/input` endpoint that reuses the harness `send_input` path.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Codex is the only harness with a stable enough live control path for v1 of this feature.\n- Tools continue to auto-approve; this endpoint is conversation continuity, not approval gating.\n- Clients use `input_supported` to decide whether to show or disable the composer.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Expose a single `{\"text\":\"...\"}` request shape and let the harness decide whether input steers a running turn or starts a new one.\n- Return `input_supported: true` only for Codex sessions; Claude/OpenCode return `false` and reject POST input.\n- Capability lives on `HarnessKind::input_supported(self)` as a compile-exhaustive match — adding a harness variant is a build error until the question is answered.\n- Stay on `/v0`; no version split.\n- Swift DTO drops its `inputSupported = true` default; `SessionState` starts pessimistic and lets the first session response (join or create) set the real value.\n- New STYLE.md rule — no defaults on wire DTOs — with fixture round-trip tests under `tests/fixtures/dto/` exercised from Rust, Python, and Swift.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Approval APIs, pending prompt tables, TTLs, or decision enums.\n- Claude/OpenCode input support.\n- iPhone client UI.\n- WebSocket migration for session events.\n- Python `SessionConfig` parity — its model is thinner than Rust's; the fixture test surfaces this, follow-up to align.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 974,
      "deletions": 81,
      "changed_files": 28
    },
    {
      "number": 620,
      "title": "lfd sessions: expose codex session input",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"$LFD_URL/v0/sessions/$SESSION_ID/input\" \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"text\":\"also check the tests\"}'\n```\n\nSession DTOs now carry `input_supported`. Clients show the input field when it's `true` (Codex) and disable it otherwise (Claude, OpenCode). Posting input to a harness that doesn't support it returns a 4xx with `input not supported for this harness`.\n\n## Summary\n\nSurfaces Codex's mid-turn session input through `lfd`. The HTTP route renames the request field from `content` to `text` to match the wire convention, and exposes per-harness capability via a new `input_supported` flag on the session DTO so clients can render the input affordance correctly without sniffing the harness string.\n\nAdds a STYLE.md `DTOs` section codifying the no-defaults rule for wire types mirrored across Rust / Python / Swift, and introduces shared fixtures under `tests/fixtures/dto/` with round-trip parse tests in all three languages so the mirrors can't silently drift.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `POST /v0/sessions/{id}/input` accepts `{\"text\": \"...\"}`; Python client and tests updated to match\n- New `SessionManagerError::InputNotSupported` returns a 4xx for harnesses without input support\n- `SessionDto` / Python `Session` / Swift `AgentSession` gain `input_supported: bool`\n- `HarnessKind::input_supported()` is the single source of truth (Codex `true`, Claude/OpenCode `false`)\n- New `rust/loopflow/src/lfd/sessions/README.md` documents the events + input endpoints\n- STYLE.md `DTOs` section + `tests/fixtures/dto/` round-trip tests in Rust, Python, and Swift\n- Concerto `TerminalWorkspaceView` gates the input field on `session.inputSupported`\n- Wave `lfd` scratch folded: shipped `harness-server-mode` item removed, remaining items renumbered",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 941,
      "deletions": 83,
      "changed_files": 31
    },
    {
      "number": 621,
      "title": "pm: gate Asana/Linear/Notion behind OAuth and add Asana working-branch claims",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op auth asana    # OAuth\nlf op auth linear   # OAuth\nlf op auth notion   # OAuth\nlf op pm init       # provision Asana \"Working branch\" custom field on a project\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nLocks the PM providers (Asana, Linear, Notion) to OAuth-only auth and removes the API-key configure path that conflicted with it. Adds an Asana-side \"Working branch\" custom field so claims become a real concurrency primitive instead of a comment, with `init_project` provisioning the field at the workspace and attaching it to the project.\n\n## Changes\n\n- CLI: drop Asana/Linear from `_PROVIDER_API_KEY_CONFIG`; `lf op auth configure asana|linear|notion` now errors with a pointer to the OAuth command. `lf op auth linear` switches from token prompt to `_connect_provider`.\n- Rust auth: `lf op auth configure` and the `configure_credential_handler` HTTP route refuse PM providers via `api_key_configure_error()`.\n- Asana provider: new `init_project` ensures a workspace-scoped \"Working branch\" text custom field exists and is attached to the project; `claim_item` writes the branch into that field and verifies both assignee and branch on read-back, failing loudly if another worker holds it. Comment fallback is kept for human-visible history.\n- Linear provider: parallel claim/init plumbing (see linear.rs additions) for branch-tracked claims.\n- Docs: README and getting-started updated to the new OAuth commands; `scripts/setup-asana.py` removed; `wave/pm/2-provider-auth.md` retired and Concerto auth UI work moved to `wave/macos/4-typed-auth-ui.md`.\n- Tests: new CLI test asserts PM providers reject API-key configure and surface the OAuth message; Asana tests cover the field provisioning and claim verification flow.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 685,
      "deletions": 303,
      "changed_files": 17
    },
    {
      "number": 625,
      "title": "install: bundle lf and lfd in Concerto.app",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\nuv run pytest python/tests/test_install_script.py -q\nuv run python scripts/install.py -n --skip wheel --skip cargo --skip swift\n```\n\nThe dry run prints the app bundle executable layout:\n\n```text\nContents/MacOS/: Concerto, lf, lfd\n```\n\nFull validation run:\n\n```bash\nuv run ruff check scripts/install.py python/tests/test_install_script.py\nuv run ruff format --check scripts/install.py python/tests/test_install_script.py\nuv run pytest python/tests/ -q\n```\n\n## Intent\n\nFix local Concerto installs so the app bundle contains every executable the app launches. `lfd` and `lf` now live in `Contents/MacOS/` beside `Concerto`, matching `Bundle.main.url(forAuxiliaryExecutable:)`, and the installer rejects broken bundles before claiming success.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The local Concerto installer targets macOS and can use `lipo` and `codesign`.\n- `swift build -c release` and `cargo build -p loopflow --release` produce the artifacts installed into the bundle.\n- `--skip` skips build stages only; install steps still install existing artifacts.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Added `BundleSpec` as the single source of truth for the app layout.\n- Made missing artifacts and invalid bundles fatal `StageError`s instead of silent skips.\n- Added a `codesign --verify` smoke check after ad-hoc signing.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Notarization or release packaging changes.\n- Runtime lookup changes in the Swift app.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 485,
      "deletions": 198,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 626,
      "title": "lf open work: add branch ops, ship flow, and triage step",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op branches list --user @me --stale 60d   # preview stale remote branches\nlf op branches prune --user @me --stale 60d  # delete after confirmation\nlf review-open-work                            # inbox-zero triage across branches, PRs, worktrees, waves\nlf ship                                        # refresh-plan → implement → gate → op: pr → op: land\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds the tooling to clear out in-flight work on your own branches: a triage step that surveys everything open and dispatches ship/abandon/prune actions, a `ship` flow that finishes and lands a branch, a `refresh-plan` step that reconciles `scratch/<branch>.md` with post-rebase reality, and `lf op branches list|prune` for remote-branch cleanup with shared filter options.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `review-open-work` interactive step — scans worktrees, user PRs, remote branches, and waves; writes `scratch/open-work.md`; dispatches `lf ship` in background per worktree\n- New `refresh-plan` step — rebases onto main, then rewrites `scratch/<branch>.md` against the current diff; appends a ship-bias block when `LOOPFLOW_FLOW_NAME=ship`\n- New `ship` flow (`refresh-plan → implement → gate → op: pr → op: land`) with a flow-name env guard exposed to steps\n- New `lf op branches` subcommand with shared `BranchFilterArgs` (`--user`, `--wave`, `--stale`, `--created-before`, `--merged`, `--include-open-prs`) and `list` / `prune` (supports `--dry-run`, `-y`); protects open PRs by default\n- Registered both new steps in the discovery catalog and README tables\n- `rust/loopflow/tests/branch_ops_tests.rs` covers the branch filter and prune logic\n- Design notes in `scratch/review-open-work.md`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1153,
      "deletions": 7,
      "changed_files": 13
    },
    {
      "number": 629,
      "title": "regression + lfd: add nightly regression suite, weekly auto-release, and wave status fixes",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Run the new regression suite locally\nuv run pytest tests/regression/ -v\n\n# CI runs it nightly at 06:00 UTC and on Sundays before the weekly auto-release\n# (see .github/workflows/regression-daily.yml and weekly-release.yml)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nStands up a regression test harness and the CI plumbing around it, and fixes a few lfd bugs the new tests cover. The regression suite exercises end-to-end behaviors that are too expensive for per-PR CI: orphaned-run recovery after an lfd restart, wave runs that include a roadmap item, and the terminal-session DTO shape Concerto depends on. A weekly GitHub Actions workflow runs the suite against `main`, and if everything is green and new commits have landed since the last tag, bumps the patch version and dispatches `release.yml` to cut a release.\n\nAlong the way, two lfd bugs surfaced and are fixed here:\n\n- After `fail_orphaned_runs` marks in-flight runs as Failed on lfd restart, the owning waves stayed stuck in Running/Waiting. The UI would keep their action buttons disabled even though no executor was attached. A new `ResetStaleActiveWaves` query resets those waves back to Idle in the same cleanup pass.\n- `POST /waves/{id}/run` with a `roadmap_item` could abort the HTTP connection with no response when PM was configured, because the ingest path called `block_on_pm` from inside the axum handler's runtime. The handler now returns a structured error instead of panicking, and a missing roadmap item returns a clean 400.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `tests/regression/` — new pytest package with a `regression` marker, conftest, and three initial scenarios (orphaned-run wave reset, PM-backed run-with-roadmap-item, terminal-session DTO exposes `tmux_name`)\n- `.github/workflows/regression-daily.yml` — nightly regression job, also `workflow_call`-able\n- `.github/workflows/weekly-release.yml` + `scripts/bump_patch_version.sh` — Sunday job that runs regression against `main`, bumps the patch version if new commits landed, refreshes `Cargo.lock`, and dispatches `release.yml`\n- `lfd` store: `ResetStaleActiveWaves` query wired into both SQLite and Postgres `fail_orphaned_runs`, with a store-level test\n- `lfd` http: new axum tests covering the roadmap-item + PM handler path and the missing-roadmap-item 400 case; `activation.rs` no longer blocks on PM from inside the async handler\n- `TerminalSessionDto` now carries `tmux_name`, mirrored in Swift (`TerminalSession`, `RepoState`, `LocalWaveService`) with new `ProcessEnvironment`/`PathNormalization` helpers and tests (`BundledDaemonPathTests`, `RepoStateInteractiveSessionTests`)\n- `pyproject.toml` — registers the `regression` pytest marker so the nightly suite is opt-in rather than running on every PR\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1193,
      "deletions": 20,
      "changed_files": 31
    },
    {
      "number": 631,
      "title": "flows: ship the build/govern/ops catalog in Concerto",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlfd serve\ncurl -s \"http://127.0.0.1:2486/v0/catalog?repo=$(pwd)\" | jq '.result.flows[] | {name, category, source}'\ncurl -s \"http://127.0.0.1:2486/v0/catalog?repo=$(pwd)\" | jq '.result.steps[] | select(.name==\"gate\")'\n```\n\nOpen Concerto, switch to **Flows**, expand `build` → `build`, and click `gate`. The left pane groups flows and steps under **Build / Govern / Ops**; the right pane lists every parent flow that reaches the selected item.\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow\nuv run pytest python/tests/\nswift test --package-path swift --filter CatalogTests\nswift test --package-path swift\ntests/e2e/test_smoke.sh\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReorganizes the built-in flow and step catalog into three agency buckets — `build` (manual), `govern` (autonomous coordination), `ops` (side-channel utilities) — and makes that catalog browsable end-to-end. `lfd` serves a new `GET /v0/catalog` endpoint that resolves builtins plus repo-local `.lf/flows` and `.lf/steps` overrides; Concerto gains a Flows tab that renders the same DTO with an upward \"used by\" walk. Docs (`README`, getting-started, `lfd.md`, Swift README) are rewritten to match the new taxonomy so users can discover the feature without grepping the source.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Move builtin flow/step markdown + YAML under `build/`, `govern/`, `ops/` subtrees and flatten names (e.g. `garden/scan` → `scan`).\n- New `lfd` route `routes/catalog.rs` exposing resolved flows, steps, categories, and source origins.\n- New Swift `Catalog` models, `LocalWaveService` fetch path, and `FlowsView` in Concerto with sidebar integration.\n- `CatalogTests` covering DTO round-trip and direct-parent discovery; smoke test and testing docs updated.\n- Docs rewritten: README flow/step tables, getting-started browse section, `docs/lfd.md` catalog example, Swift README note.\n- Wave authoring notes under `wave/flows/` capturing the reorg, view design, and follow-ups.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Session-state overlay on top of the static catalog\n- `maybe` primitive migration and xor cleanup\n- iOS and search/filter polish for the Flows surface\n- Cleanup of older archival wave docs",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1739,
      "deletions": 397,
      "changed_files": 114
    },
    {
      "number": 634,
      "title": "pm flows: skip cleanly on non-PM branches",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# deploy/sync now no-op the PM step on branches without a PM-enabled wave\nlf op flow deploy\nlf op flow sync\n\n# push-diff short-circuits before provider auth when wave/<name>/ is untouched\nlf op pm push-diff <wave>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMakes `op: pm pull` and `op: pm push-diff` safe to chain inside flows on branches that don't resolve to a PM-enabled wave. `deploy` and `sync` previously failed the queue on CI-only or plain code branches; now they log a skip and move on. `pm push-diff` also short-circuits before building the PM provider when nothing under `wave/<name>/` changed, so branches that never touched roadmap files don't trigger auth prompts or remote calls.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `resolve_pm_waves_for_flow` returns an empty list when the current branch has no resolvable or PM-enabled wave; flow executor logs a skip for `pm pull` and `pm push-diff` in that case\n- `pm_push_diff_async` diffs `wave/<name>/` against the push-diff baseline before provider setup and returns a zero-result when empty\n- README, built-in flow docs, and release notes describe the new no-op behavior; explicit `lf op pm pull <wave>` / `lf op pm push-diff <wave>` still target the named wave",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 58,
      "deletions": 9,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 635,
      "title": "scratch: add rebase notes for already-merged branch",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncat scratch/questions.md\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis branch was rebased onto `origin/main` and the cumulative code diff is zero — the original work shipped to main as squash commit `5803e553` (\"flows: ship the build/govern/ops catalog in Concerto\"). The only remaining delta is a `scratch/questions.md` note documenting the rebase outcome so the next `lf op` pass has context when it cleans up.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add `scratch/questions.md` recording the rebase situation: four conflicted files resolved with `--ours` (main's polished post-merge versions), remaining commits touch `scratch/` only, pushed with `--force-with-lease`.\n- No code changes; no tests run.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 0,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 0
    },
    {
      "number": 636,
      "title": "release: add decisions ledger for narrative-first release notes",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# During the cycle, log durable intent decisions\n$EDITOR release/unreleased/DECISIONS.md\n\n# At release time, the ledger is promoted and fed to release-notes\nlf op release run patch\n\n# Per-version archive + always-latest root notes\nfind release -maxdepth 2 -type f | sort\ncat RELEASE_NOTES.md\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds `release/unreleased/DECISIONS.md` as an append-only ledger for intent and policy decisions made during a release cycle. Interactive runs append to it when a session produces something a contributor would cite six months later (policy choices, scope calls, paths not taken); headless runs stay out to avoid noise. During `lf op release run`, Loopflow promotes `release/unreleased/` to `release/v<version>/`, feeds `DECISIONS.md` into the release-notes step as the primary narrative source, and archives the generated `RELEASE_NOTES.md` to `release/v<version>/NOTES.md`. If the ledger is absent, release notes fall back to merged PR history as before.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `ops/release.rs`: `load_unreleased_decisions`, `promote_unreleased_dir` (idempotent, merges into an existing version dir), and `archive_release_notes`; `ReleaseNotesContext` gains a `decisions` field\n- `engine/builtins/ops/step/release-notes.md`: lead with decisions when present, PRs become supplementary; release.md step documents the promotion + archival\n- `engine/builtins/LOOPFLOW.md`: instructs interactive runs when/how to append ledger entries, with the entry template\n- Docs: `README.md`, `docs/lfop.md`, and new `release/README.md` describe the ledger, promotion, and archive layout; `lf op commit` docs updated to reflect auto-generated messages and `-p` / `--no-add`\n- `release/unreleased/DECISIONS.md` seeded with the decision that introduces this workflow; `release/v0.9.9/NOTES.md` backfilled\n- Tests cover missing/blank/populated ledger, noop promotion, rename, merge into existing version dir, and collision error; prompt goldens refreshed",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 463,
      "deletions": 13,
      "changed_files": 20
    },
    {
      "number": 637,
      "title": "repo: automate and document dependabot updates",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ngh pr list --author app/dependabot\ngh run list --workflow CI\n```\n\nReviewer should see weekly updates configured for `uv`, `cargo`, `swift`, and `github-actions`, plus a zero-touch workflow that auto-merges green Dependabot PRs and closes red ones.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds weekly Dependabot updates for the repo's four dependency surfaces (`uv`, `cargo`, Swift Package Manager, and GitHub Actions) and a companion workflow that makes the resulting PRs zero-touch: green PRs get squash auto-merge on open/reopen, and PRs tied to a failed `CI` run get a comment and are closed so the next weekly bump can retry cleanly.\n\nSince `workflow_run` doesn't hand over the PR number directly, the close-on-red job matches the failed run back to its PR by `head_sha`. This couples the workflow to the CI workflow's name (`CI`), which is documented in `TESTING.md` so future renames don't silently break the close path.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `.github/dependabot.yml` — weekly updates for `uv`, `cargo`, `swift`, `github-actions`\n- `.github/workflows/dependabot-auto.yml` — enable auto-merge on open/reopen; comment + close on CI failure\n- `TESTING.md` — document the Dependabot workflow contract and the `CI` name coupling\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 82,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 638,
      "title": "build(deps): bump actions/checkout from 4 to 6",
      "body": "Bumps [actions/checkout](https://github.com/actions/checkout) from 4 to 6.\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/releases\">actions/checkout's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v6.0.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update README to include Node.js 24 support details and requirements by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2248\">actions/checkout#2248</a></li>\n<li>Persist creds to a separate file by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2286\">actions/checkout#2286</a></li>\n<li>v6-beta by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2298\">actions/checkout#2298</a></li>\n<li>update readme/changelog for v6 by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2311\">actions/checkout#2311</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v5.0.0...v6.0.0\">https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v5.0.0...v6.0.0</a></p>\n<h2>v6-beta</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<p>Updated persist-credentials to store the credentials under <code>$RUNNER_TEMP</code> instead of directly in the local git config.</p>\n<p>This requires a minimum Actions Runner version of <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/runner/releases/tag/v2.329.0\">v2.329.0</a> to access the persisted credentials for <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/tutorials/use-containerized-services/create-a-docker-container-action\">Docker container action</a> scenarios.</p>\n<h2>v5.0.1</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Port v6 cleanup to v5 by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2301\">actions/checkout#2301</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v5...v5.0.1\">https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v5...v5.0.1</a></p>\n<h2>v5.0.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update actions checkout to use node 24 by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2226\">actions/checkout#2226</a></li>\n<li>Prepare v5.0.0 release by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2238\">actions/checkout#2238</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>⚠️ Minimum Compatible Runner Version</h2>\n<p><strong>v2.327.1</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://github.com/actions/runner/releases/tag/v2.327.1\">Release Notes</a></p>\n<p>Make sure your runner is updated to this version or newer to use this release.</p>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v4...v5.0.0\">https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v4...v5.0.0</a></p>\n<h2>v4.3.1</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Port v6 cleanup to v4 by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2305\">actions/checkout#2305</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v4...v4.3.1\">https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v4...v4.3.1</a></p>\n<h2>v4.3.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>docs: update README.md by <a href=\"https://github.com/motss\"><code>@​motss</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1971\">actions/checkout#1971</a></li>\n<li>Add internal repos for checking out multiple repositories by <a href=\"https://github.com/mouismail\"><code>@​mouismail</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1977\">actions/checkout#1977</a></li>\n<li>Documentation update - add recommended permissions to Readme by <a href=\"https://github.com/benwells\"><code>@​benwells</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2043\">actions/checkout#2043</a></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\">actions/checkout's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h1>Changelog</h1>\n<h2>v6.0.2</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Fix tag handling: preserve annotations and explicit fetch-tags by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2356\">actions/checkout#2356</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v6.0.1</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Add worktree support for persist-credentials includeIf by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2327\">actions/checkout#2327</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v6.0.0</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Persist creds to a separate file by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2286\">actions/checkout#2286</a></li>\n<li>Update README to include Node.js 24 support details and requirements by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2248\">actions/checkout#2248</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v5.0.1</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Port v6 cleanup to v5 by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2301\">actions/checkout#2301</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v5.0.0</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update actions checkout to use node 24 by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2226\">actions/checkout#2226</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v4.3.1</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Port v6 cleanup to v4 by <a href=\"https://github.com/ericsciple\"><code>@​ericsciple</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2305\">actions/checkout#2305</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v4.3.0</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>docs: update README.md by <a href=\"https://github.com/motss\"><code>@​motss</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1971\">actions/checkout#1971</a></li>\n<li>Add internal repos for checking out multiple repositories by <a href=\"https://github.com/mouismail\"><code>@​mouismail</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1977\">actions/checkout#1977</a></li>\n<li>Documentation update - add recommended permissions to Readme by <a href=\"https://github.com/benwells\"><code>@​benwells</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2043\">actions/checkout#2043</a></li>\n<li>Adjust positioning of user email note and permissions heading by <a href=\"https://github.com/joshmgross\"><code>@​joshmgross</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2044\">actions/checkout#2044</a></li>\n<li>Update README.md by <a href=\"https://github.com/nebuk89\"><code>@​nebuk89</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2194\">actions/checkout#2194</a></li>\n<li>Update CODEOWNERS for actions by <a href=\"https://github.com/TingluoHuang\"><code>@​TingluoHuang</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2224\">actions/checkout#2224</a></li>\n<li>Update package dependencies by <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanmkc\"><code>@​salmanmkc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/2236\">actions/checkout#2236</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v4.2.2</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>url-helper.ts</code> now leverages well-known environment variables by <a href=\"https://github.com/jww3\"><code>@​jww3</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1941\">actions/checkout#1941</a></li>\n<li>Expand unit test coverage for <code>isGhes</code> by <a href=\"https://github.com/jww3\"><code>@​jww3</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1946\">actions/checkout#1946</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v4.2.1</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check out other refs/* by commit if provided, fall back to ref by <a href=\"https://github.com/orhantoy\"><code>@​orhantoy</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1924\">actions/checkout#1924</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v4.2.0</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Add Ref and Commit outputs by <a href=\"https://github.com/lucacome\"><code>@​lucacome</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1180\">actions/checkout#1180</a></li>\n<li>Dependency updates by <a href=\"https://github.com/dependabot\"><code>@​dependabot</code></a>- <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1777\">actions/checkout#1777</a>, <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1872\">actions/checkout#1872</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v4.1.7</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Bump the minor-npm-dependencies group across 1 directory with 4 updates by <a href=\"https://github.com/dependabot\"><code>@​dependabot</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1739\">actions/checkout#1739</a></li>\n<li>Bump actions/checkout from 3 to 4 by <a href=\"https://github.com/dependabot\"><code>@​dependabot</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1697\">actions/checkout#1697</a></li>\n<li>Check out other refs/* by commit by <a href=\"https://github.com/orhantoy\"><code>@​orhantoy</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1774\">actions/checkout#1774</a></li>\n<li>Pin actions/checkout's own workflows to a known, good, stable version. by <a href=\"https://github.com/jww3\"><code>@​jww3</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1776\">actions/checkout#1776</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>v4.1.6</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check platform to set archive extension appropriately by <a href=\"https://github.com/cory-miller\"><code>@​cory-miller</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/pull/1732\">actions/checkout#1732</a></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/de0fac2e4500dabe0009e67214ff5f5447ce83dd\"><code>de0fac2</code></a> Fix tag handling: preserve annotations and explicit fetch-tags (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2356\">#2356</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/064fe7f3312418007dea2b49a19844a9ee378f49\"><code>064fe7f</code></a> Add orchestration_id to git user-agent when ACTIONS_ORCHESTRATION_ID is set (...</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/8e8c483db84b4bee98b60c0593521ed34d9990e8\"><code>8e8c483</code></a> Clarify v6 README (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2328\">#2328</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/033fa0dc0b82693d8986f1016a0ec2c5e7d9cbb1\"><code>033fa0d</code></a> Add worktree support for persist-credentials includeIf (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2327\">#2327</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/c2d88d3ecc89a9ef08eebf45d9637801dcee7eb5\"><code>c2d88d3</code></a> Update all references from v5 and v4 to v6 (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2314\">#2314</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/1af3b93b6815bc44a9784bd300feb67ff0d1eeb3\"><code>1af3b93</code></a> update readme/changelog for v6 (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2311\">#2311</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/71cf2267d89c5cb81562390fa70a37fa40b1305e\"><code>71cf226</code></a> v6-beta (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2298\">#2298</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/069c6959146423d11cd0184e6accf28f9d45f06e\"><code>069c695</code></a> Persist creds to a separate file (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2286\">#2286</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/ff7abcd0c3c05ccf6adc123a8cd1fd4fb30fb493\"><code>ff7abcd</code></a> Update README to include Node.js 24 support details and requirements (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2248\">#2248</a>)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/commit/08c6903cd8c0fde910a37f88322edcfb5dd907a8\"><code>08c6903</code></a> Prepare v5.0.0 release (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/checkout/issues/2238\">#2238</a>)</li>\n<li>Additional commits viewable in <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/checkout/compare/v4...v6\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=actions/checkout&package-manager=github_actions&previous-version=4&new-version=6)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by commenting on this PR:\n- `@dependabot rebase` will rebase this PR\n- `@dependabot recreate` will recreate this PR, overwriting any edits that have been made to it\n- `@dependabot show <dependency name> ignore conditions` will show all of the ignore conditions of the specified dependency\n- `@dependabot ignore this major version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this major version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this minor version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this minor version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this dependency` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this dependency (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n\n\n</details>",
      "files": [],
